/* Mock final exams: last lesson of each course (id COURSE:xmock). Kept apart from content.js so the weekly Word sync never overwrites it. */
const MOCKS={"BUS1103":{"title":"Mock Final Exam","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Last lesson of the course: a full mock final exam, built from the announced format, the briefing and the Moodle material."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Format"},{"t":"p","x":"Format taken from the announcement on Moodle (24 Nov): 3 hours, closed book, calculator allowed, 10 multiple-choice questions and 6 problems (some with several parts). Coverage: chapters not covered in the mid-term, i.e. Chapters 7 to 11 plus the statement of cash flows (Chapter 12, covered in the last lecture). Chapter 5 is excluded. Show all workings. Assume a perpetual inventory system, a December 31 year-end and no income tax unless stated. Part C is extra drill and is not in the real paper. 100 marks: Part A 20, Part B 80."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"How to work it"},{"t":"li","x":"Open the Exercises tab. Work under exam conditions: closed book, time limit 3 hours, closed book, calculator."},{"t":"li","x":"Type your answer in the box (or on paper), then tap Show answer to see the model answer and marking guide. Mark yourself Got it or Missed."},{"t":"li","x":"Multiple choice is checked instantly."},{"t":"li","x":"Writing parts: paste your answer in the chat with Claude and ask for a correction against the marks."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Time plan"},{"t":"li","x":"Part A: 20 marks, about 20 minutes (2 marks each)."},{"t":"li","x":"Part B: 80 marks, about 2 h 20 min: roughly 1.75 minutes per mark. Show all workings; follow-through marks are given."},{"t":"li","x":"Tables in the answers can be practised with \"Fill in the table\" where offered."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part A: Multiple choice (10 questions, 2 marks each)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **Unit costs are rising throughout the year and inventory quantities are stable. Compared with weighted average, FIFO will report:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Lower ending inventory and lower net income"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Higher ending inventory and higher net income"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Higher ending inventory and lower net income"},{"t":"p","x":"D. The same ending inventory and net income"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **B — FIFO sends the old, cheap costs to COGS and keeps the newest costs in inventory."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **Ending inventory has a cost of 40,000 and a net realizable value of 36,000. The write-down:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Decreases net income by 4,000 and is shown as a loss below gross profit"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Is recorded only in the year the goods are sold"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Increases cost of goods sold by 4,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Is not recorded because NRV is estimated"},{"t":"a","x":"**2. **C — LCNRV: the holding loss is added to COGS in the period the value falls, so net income falls by 4,000."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Equipment costs 100,000, residual value 10,000, life 5 years. Double-declining-balance depreciation for YEAR 2 is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 36,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 24,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 40,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 18,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**3. **B — DDB rate 40%: year 1 = 40,000; NBV 60,000; year 2 = 24,000."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **A lawsuit loss is probable and can be reasonably estimated at 200,000. Under both US GAAP and IFRS the company:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Records a liability and an expense"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Only discloses the matter in the notes"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Records nothing, as nothing is paid yet"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Records a gain contingency"},{"t":"a","x":"**4. **A — Probable plus estimable: record."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Bonds are sold when the market rate equals the coupon rate. The bonds are issued:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. At a premium"},{"t":"p","x":"B. At a discount"},{"t":"p","x":"C. With no interest"},{"t":"p","x":"D. At par"},{"t":"a","x":"**5. **D — Equal rates: price equals face value."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **A company buys back its own shares for cash. Total stockholders' equity:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Decreases by the cost of the shares"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Increases, because shares are assets"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Is unchanged"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Decreases only if the price is above par"},{"t":"a","x":"**6. **A — Treasury stock is a contra-equity account."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **A 5% (small) stock dividend is declared. Compared with before, total stockholders' equity:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Rises by the market value of the shares"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Is unchanged, with RE transferred to contributed capital"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Falls by the market value of the shares"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Is unchanged, because nothing is recorded"},{"t":"a","x":"**7. **B — The entry moves amounts from retained earnings to common stock and APIC."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **Dividends paid in cash are classified in the statement of cash flows as:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Operating"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Investing"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Financing"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Not reported"},{"t":"a","x":"**8. **C — Dividends are financing."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **Accounts receivable increased by 40,000 during the year. In the indirect method, operating cash flow is adjusted by:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Adding 40,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. No adjustment"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Subtracting 40,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Subtracting 40,000 in investing"},{"t":"a","x":"**9. **C — Revenue not yet collected: subtract the increase."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **Free cash flow is commonly computed as:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Net income less dividends"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Total cash less total debt"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Operating cash flow less capex"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Investing less financing cash flow"},{"t":"a","x":"**10. **C — CFO minus capex."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part B: Problems (80 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Inventory and cost of goods sold (Chapter 7) (16 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"Kowloon Sound Ltd sells portable speakers and uses the **perpetual** system. The records for January show:"},{"t":"li","x":"Jan 1 — Event: Beginning inventory · Units: 100 · Unit cost / price: cost $20"},{"t":"li","x":"Jan 5 — Event: Purchase · Units: 200 · Unit cost / price: cost $22"},{"t":"li","x":"Jan 12 — Event: Sale · Units: 150 · Unit cost / price: price $50"},{"t":"li","x":"Jan 20 — Event: Purchase · Units: 150 · Unit cost / price: cost $24"},{"t":"li","x":"Jan 28 — Event: Sale · Units: 220 · Unit cost / price: price $52"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Calculate cost of goods sold, ending inventory and gross profit for January under (i) **FIFO** and (ii) **weighted average (perpetual)**. Give unit costs to the nearest cent and total amounts to the nearest dollar. (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** On January 31 the net realizable value of the speakers in ending inventory is $21 per unit. Using the FIFO figures, apply the **lower of cost or net realizable value** rule: give the amount of the write-down, the journal entry, and the effect on gross profit. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** Prices are rising. State which method gives the higher net income and which gives the higher ending inventory, and explain why a company must use its method consistently. (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** Separate event. On March 3 the company buys goods with an invoice of $8,000, terms 2/10, n/30, FOB shipping point. It pays $250 freight to a carrier on delivery, returns $1,000 of goods on March 6, and pays the supplier on March 12. Prepare the journal entries for March 3, March 6, the freight, and March 12, and state the final cost of the inventory. (3)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (8)**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","FIFO","Weighted average (perpetual)"],["Jan 12 sale, 150 units","100 × 20 + 50 × 22 = **3,100**","Avg after Jan 5 purchase = (2,000 + 4,400) / 300 = $21.33; 150 × 21.33 = **3,200**"],["Jan 28 sale, 220 units","150 × 22 + 70 × 24 = 3,300 + 1,680 = **4,980**","Avg after Jan 20 purchase = (3,200 + 3,600) / 300 = $22.67; 220 × 22.67 = **4,987**"],["**COGS**","**8,080**","**8,187** (3,200 + 4,987)"],["**Ending inventory** (80 units)","80 × 24 = **1,920**","80 × 22.67 = **1,813**"],["Sales","150 × 50 + 220 × 52 = 7,500 + 11,440 = **18,940**","same"],["**Gross profit**","**10,860**","**10,753**"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Check: goods available = 2,000 + 4,400 + 3,600 = 10,000 = COGS + ending inventory in both methods (8,080 + 1,920; 8,187 + 1,813). Marks: each COGS 2, ending inventory 1, gross profit 1.\n**(b) (3)** FIFO ending inventory 80 × 24 = 1,920; NRV 80 × 21 = 1,680; write-down **240**. Entry: Dr Cost of goods sold 240 / Cr Inventory 240. Gross profit falls from 10,860 to **10,620**. No gain would be recorded if NRV exceeded cost. (Conservatism.)\n**(c) (2)** With rising prices FIFO gives the **higher net income** (older, lower costs in COGS) and the **higher ending inventory** (newest costs). Consistency: the same method each period so that results are comparable across years; changes must be disclosed with a reason.\n**(d) (3)** Mar 3: Dr Inventory 8,000 / Cr Accounts payable 8,000. Freight (paid by buyer, FOB shipping point): Dr Inventory 250 / Cr Cash 250. Mar 6: Dr Accounts payable 1,000 / Cr Inventory 1,000. Mar 12 (within 10 days; discount 2% × 7,000 = 140): Dr Accounts payable 7,000 / Cr Cash 6,860, Cr Inventory 140. **Final inventory cost = 8,000 + 250 − 1,000 − 140 = 7,110.**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Property, plant and equipment, intangibles and natural resources (Chapter 8) (16 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Delta Manufacturing bought a machine on **January 1, Year 1**. Costs: purchase price $150,000; import duty $9,000; freight $4,000; installation $6,000; testing $2,000; first-year insurance $3,000; and $1,500 to repair a scratch caused while unloading. Estimated residual value $11,000; useful life 5 years or 400,000 machine-hours. (i) Calculate the **acquisition cost**. (ii) Calculate depreciation for Year 1 under straight-line and under units-of-production, if the machine ran 70,000 hours. (iii) Calculate depreciation for Years 1 and 2 under double-declining-balance. (7)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** At the start of Year 3 management estimates that the machine will last **6 years in total** (not 5) and that the residual value is **$7,000**. Using straight-line and the acquisition cost from (a), calculate Year 3 depreciation. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** At the end of Year 4 the machine is sold for **$70,000** cash. Calculate the net book value and the gain or loss and give the journal entry. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** Separate items. (i) A truck costing $48,000 with residual value $3,000 and life 5 years is bought on **October 1** (straight-line). Depreciation for the year ended December 31? (1.5) (ii) A patent is purchased for $90,000: legal life 15 years, expected economic life 10 years. Annual amortisation? (0.5) (iii) A mine costs $2,400,000 and has 1,200,000 tonnes of estimated reserves; 150,000 tonnes are extracted and 120,000 tonnes are sold. Give the depletion for the period, how much goes to cost of goods sold and how much remains in inventory. (1)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (7)** (i) Acquisition cost = 150,000 + 9,000 + 4,000 + 6,000 + 2,000 = **171,000**. The insurance (3,000) and the scratch repair (1,500) are expenses. (2) (ii) Straight-line: (171,000 − 11,000) / 5 = **32,000**. Units-of-production: rate = 160,000 / 400,000 = $0.40 per hour; 70,000 × 0.40 = **28,000**. (2.5) (iii) DDB rate = 2 × 1/5 = 40%: Year 1 = 171,000 × 40% = **68,400**; NBV 102,600; Year 2 = 102,600 × 40% = **41,040**. Residual ignored except as the final floor. (2.5)\n**(b) (3)** After two years straight-line: accumulated depreciation 64,000; NBV 107,000. Change in estimate is **prospective**: remaining life 6 − 2 = 4 years; Year 3 depreciation = (107,000 − 7,000) / 4 = **25,000**. No restatement of prior years.\n**(c) (3)** Record Year 4 depreciation first (25,000). Accumulated depreciation = 64,000 + 25,000 + 25,000 = 114,000; NBV = 171,000 − 114,000 = **57,000**. Proceeds 70,000 − 57,000 = **gain 13,000**. Entry: Dr Cash 70,000; Dr Accumulated depreciation 114,000 / Cr Machine 171,000; Cr Gain on disposal 13,000.\n**(d) (3)** (i) (48,000 − 3,000) / 5 = 9,000 per year × 3/12 = **2,250**. (ii) Use the shorter of legal and economic life: 90,000 / 10 = **9,000**. (iii) Depletion rate = 2,400,000 / 1,200,000 = $2 per tonne; extracted 150,000 × 2 = **300,000**; sold 120,000 × 2 = **240,000** in COGS; **60,000** remains in inventory."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Current liabilities and contingencies (Chapter 9) (12 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"At December 31, Ocean Retail has current assets of $900,000 and accounts payable and other current liabilities of $260,000 before the following items:"},{"t":"li","x":"1. On **November 1** it borrowed $240,000 at 9% for one year; interest is payable at maturity."},{"t":"li","x":"2. It has a long-term note of $600,000, repayable at $100,000 on each January 1, starting next January 1."},{"t":"li","x":"3. It sold gift cards of $50,000 in November and December; $18,000 have been redeemed by December 31."},{"t":"li","x":"4. Four legal matters: **A**, loss probable (70%), estimate $200,000; **B**, 60% chance of a loss, estimate $150,000; **C**, loss only reasonably possible, estimate $90,000; **D**, remote."},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Prepare the journal entries at November 1 and December 31 for item 1, and state the total interest expense that will be recorded over the life of the note. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** Show the reclassification for item 2 and the amount of the gift-card liability (item 3). (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** For each of A to D, state whether the company records a liability, only discloses, or does nothing under **(i) US GAAP** and **(ii) IFRS**, and explain the difference. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** Calculate total current liabilities and the **current ratio**, treating item 1 as current and recording only matter A among the lawsuits (US GAAP). Then say how the ratio would change if the accrual of interest and the reclassification had been forgotten. (3)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (3)** Nov 1: Dr Cash 240,000 / Cr Notes payable 240,000. Dec 31: interest = 240,000 × 9% × 2/12 = 3,600: Dr Interest expense 3,600 / Cr Interest payable 3,600. Total interest over the life = 240,000 × 9% = **21,600** (the remaining 18,000 will be expensed next year).\n**(b) (2)** Reclassify 100,000 as current (current portion of long-term debt); the remaining 500,000 stays long-term. Gift-card liability: deferred revenue = 50,000 − 18,000 = **32,000**; the 18,000 redeemed is revenue.\n**(c) (4)** A: probable and estimable: record 200,000 under both. B: US GAAP (probable about 70% or more): 60% is not probable, so **disclose** only; IFRS (\"more likely than not\", over 50%): 60% is probable, so **record** 150,000 (if a reliable estimate exists). C: reasonably possible: **disclose** under both; no liability. D: remote: **nothing**. The difference is the probability threshold (GAAP stricter, so fewer liabilities recorded).\n**(d) (3)** Current liabilities = 260,000 + 240,000 + 3,600 + 100,000 + 32,000 + 200,000 = **835,600**. Current ratio = 900,000 / 835,600 = **1.08**. Without the accrual and the reclassification: 260,000 + 240,000 + 32,000 + 200,000 = 732,000; ratio = 900,000 / 732,000 = 1.23: liquidity overstated (and net income overstated by 3,600)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Bonds payable (Chapter 10) (12 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"On **January 1, Year 1**, Harbour Energy issues bonds with face value **$2,000,000**, coupon **8%**, interest paid **June 30 and December 31**, maturity in 5 years. The market rate is 8%."},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Explain why the bonds are issued at par, then prepare the journal entries for the issue and for the first two interest payments, and show how interest expense is reported on the income statement. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** Extension (not in the lectures; for challenge). Suppose instead the **market rate is 10%** (5% per half-year). Using the factors **7.7217** (annuity, 5%, 10 periods) and **0.6139** (single sum, 5%, 10 periods), calculate the issue price and the discount, and explain why the bonds sell at a discount. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** Harbour Energy has 400,000 shares outstanding, earns operating income of **$1,000,000** before interest and tax and pays 25% tax. Compare earnings per share if it (Plan A) issues the bonds at par or (Plan B) issues 100,000 new shares at $20. Which is better and why? (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** List two advantages and two disadvantages of issuing bonds compared with issuing shares. (2)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (4)** Market rate = coupon rate, so the present value of the future cash flows (the principal plus the interest annuity) equals the face value: issue at par, price 2,000,000. Jan 1: Dr Cash 2,000,000 / Cr Bonds payable 2,000,000. Jun 30 and Dec 31: interest = 2,000,000 × 8% × 6/12 = 80,000: Dr Interest expense 80,000 / Cr Cash 80,000 (each date). Interest expense is reported separately **below operating income** because it relates to financing, not operations.\n**(b) (3)** Issue price = 80,000 × 7.7217 + 2,000,000 × 0.6139 = 617,736 + 1,227,800 = **1,845,536**. Discount = 2,000,000 − 1,845,536 = **154,464**. The bonds sell at a discount because the coupon (4% per half-year) is below the market rate (5%): investors pay less so that their effective return equals the market rate.\n**(c) (3)** Plan A: interest 160,000; income before tax 840,000; tax 25% = 210,000; net income 630,000; EPS = 630,000 / 400,000 = **$1.575**. Plan B: net income 1,000,000 × 75% = 750,000; shares 500,000; EPS = **$1.50**. Plan A is better: the after-tax cost of debt (8% × 0.75 = 6%) is lower than the return earned on the funds, and no new shares dilute existing holders (positive leverage); the risk is a fixed obligation if earnings fall.\n**(d) (2)** Advantages: stockholders keep control (bondholders do not vote); interest is tax-deductible; leverage can raise returns. Disadvantages: interest must be paid whether or not there is profit (bankruptcy risk); principal must be repaid or refinanced at maturity (cash-flow burden)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Stockholders' equity (Chapter 11) (14 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"Asia Pacific Foods has, at January 1: common stock ($1 par, 1,000,000 shares issued and outstanding) $1,000,000; additional paid-in capital $14,000,000; retained earnings $6,000,000. During the year:"},{"t":"li","x":"1. **Feb 1:** issues 200,000 shares at $18."},{"t":"li","x":"2. **Mar 1:** buys back 50,000 shares at $20."},{"t":"li","x":"3. **Jun 1:** reissues 20,000 treasury shares at $23."},{"t":"li","x":"4. **Sep 1:** reissues 15,000 treasury shares at $15."},{"t":"li","x":"5. **Oct 1:** declares a cash dividend of $0.40 per share (payable Nov 1, record date Oct 15)."},{"t":"li","x":"6. **Nov 15:** declares and distributes a 5% stock dividend when the market price is $22."},{"t":"li","x":"7. Net income for the year is $2,300,000."},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Prepare journal entries for transactions 1 to 6, stating the number of shares outstanding used for each dividend. (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** Prepare the stockholders' equity section at December 31. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** Separate. The company also has 20,000 shares of 6%, $50 par cumulative preferred stock; dividends are one year in arrears. If a total dividend of $300,000 is declared, how much goes to preferred and common? Then explain the difference between a **stock dividend** and a **2-for-1 stock split**. (2)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (8)**\n(1) Dr Cash 3,600,000 / Cr Common stock 200,000, Cr APIC 3,400,000.\n(2) Dr Treasury stock 1,000,000 / Cr Cash 1,000,000 (50,000 × 20).\n(3) Dr Cash 460,000 / Cr Treasury stock 400,000, Cr APIC (treasury stock) 60,000 (20,000 × 23; cost 20,000 × 20).\n(4) Dr Cash 225,000; Dr APIC (treasury stock) 60,000; Dr Retained earnings 15,000 / Cr Treasury stock 300,000 (15,000 × 20; proceeds 15,000 × 15 = 225,000; shortfall 75,000, of which 60,000 is absorbed by the APIC balance and 15,000 by retained earnings).\n(5) Shares outstanding = 1,000,000 + 200,000 − 50,000 + 20,000 + 15,000 = **1,185,000**. Declaration (Oct 1): Dr Retained earnings 474,000 / Cr Dividends payable 474,000 (1,185,000 × 0.40). Record date Oct 15: no entry. Payment (Nov 1): Dr Dividends payable 474,000 / Cr Cash 474,000.\n(6) Small stock dividend (below 20-25%): at market value. Shares = 5% × 1,185,000 = **59,250**; Dr Retained earnings 1,303,500 (59,250 × 22) / Cr Common stock 59,250, Cr APIC 1,244,250.\n**(b) (4)** Common stock: 1,000,000 + 200,000 + 59,250 = **1,259,250**. APIC: 14,000,000 + 3,400,000 + 60,000 − 60,000 + 1,244,250 = **18,644,250**. Retained earnings: 6,000,000 + 2,300,000 − 15,000 − 474,000 − 1,303,500 = **6,507,500**. Less treasury stock (15,000 shares at cost 20): **(300,000)**. **Total stockholders' equity = 26,111,000.**\n**(c) (2)** Current preference = 20,000 × 50 × 6% = 60,000 per year. Arrears 60,000 + current 60,000 = **120,000 to preferred**; common receives 300,000 − 120,000 = **180,000**. A stock dividend moves amounts from retained earnings to contributed capital (market value if small, par if large), so total equity is unchanged but the number of shares rises; a split changes the number of shares and par per share (2-for-1: shares double, par halves, total par unchanged) with **no journal entry**."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Statement of cash flows (Chapter 12) (10 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"Pearl River Trading's data for the year (in $000):"},{"t":"li","x":"Net income 420; depreciation expense 85; **loss on sale of equipment 8** (equipment with net book value 30 was sold for 22 cash)."},{"t":"li","x":"Changes: accounts receivable **up** 40; inventory **down** 15; prepaid expenses **up** 5; accounts payable **up** 30; accrued liabilities **down** 10; income taxes payable **up** 6."},{"t":"li","x":"Purchase of equipment (cash) 140; purchase of investments 50."},{"t":"li","x":"Issued shares for cash 100; repaid bank loan 60; issued bonds for cash 200; dividends paid 90; bought treasury stock 30."},{"t":"li","x":"Equipment worth 80 was also acquired by issuing a long-term note."},{"t":"li","x":"Cash at the beginning of the year: 110."},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Prepare the statement of cash flows using the **indirect method**, with the three sections, the net change in cash and ending cash. (7)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** Explain why the loss on sale is added back and why the proceeds are not in operating activities; how should the equipment acquired through the note be reported; and calculate **free cash flow**. (3)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (7) ($000)**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Operating activities",""],["Net income","420"],["Depreciation","+85"],["Loss on sale of equipment","+8"],["Accounts receivable increase","−40"],["Inventory decrease","+15"],["Prepaid expenses increase","−5"],["Accounts payable increase","+30"],["Accrued liabilities decrease","−10"],["Income taxes payable increase","+6"],["**Net cash from operating activities**","**509**"],["Investing: purchase of equipment","−140"],["Investing: sale of equipment","+22"],["Investing: purchase of investments","−50"],["**Net cash from investing activities**","**−168**"],["Financing: shares issued","+100"],["Financing: bank loan repaid","−60"],["Financing: bonds issued","+200"],["Financing: dividends paid","−90"],["Financing: treasury stock purchased","−30"],["**Net cash from financing activities**","**+120**"],["**Net increase in cash**","**461**"],["Beginning cash","110"],["**Ending cash**","**571**"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(b) (3)** The loss is a non-cash expense that reduced net income but used no cash, so it is added back; the actual cash received (22) belongs to investing because the sale of equipment is an investing event, not part of operations. The equipment acquired with a note (80) involves no cash: report it in a separate **non-cash investing and financing activities** disclosure. Free cash flow = operating cash flow − capital expenditures = 509 − 140 = **369**."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part C: Extra drill (21 more multiple choice)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **A merchandiser buys goods FOB shipping point. Who owns the goods while they are in transit, and who normally pays the freight?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Buyer owns them; buyer pays"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Seller owns them; seller pays"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Seller owns them; buyer pays"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Buyer owns them; seller pays"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **A — FOB shipping point: title passes at the seller's dock, so the buyer owns the goods in transit and pays the freight (added to inventory cost)."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **Under the perpetual system, goods bought on account at 8,000 are partly returned at 1,000. The return entry is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Dr Inventory 1,000 / Cr Accounts payable 1,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Dr Accounts payable 1,000 / Cr Cost of goods sold 1,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Dr Purchase returns 1,000 / Cr Cash 1,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Dr Accounts payable 1,000 / Cr Inventory 1,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**2. **D — The liability and the inventory both fall."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Which of these is capitalised (added to the asset's cost) rather than expensed?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Ordinary repairs to a delivery van each year"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Installation of a new machine"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Annual painting of an office"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Insurance on the machine in year two"},{"t":"a","x":"**3. **B — Costs to get the asset ready for use are capitalised; ordinary repairs and insurance are expenses."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **An asset with a net book value of 57,000 is sold for 70,000. The company reports:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. A loss of 13,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. A gain of 70,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. A loss of 57,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. A gain of 13,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**4. **D — Proceeds above NBV = gain."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **A company revises the useful life of a machine. The change in estimate is applied:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Retroactively, restating prior years"},{"t":"p","x":"B. By correcting prior-year net income"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Prospectively, over the remaining life"},{"t":"p","x":"D. In the year of disposal only"},{"t":"a","x":"**5. **C — Changes in estimate are prospective."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **Under IFRS a 60% chance of losing a lawsuit is 'probable'. Under US GAAP, the same case is usually:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Recorded as a liability"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Remote"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Disclosed if estimable or possible"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Ignored completely"},{"t":"a","x":"**6. **C — US GAAP needs a higher threshold (about 70%), so a 60% case is typically only disclosed."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **On December 31, a 600,000 long-term note requires 100,000 to be repaid on January 1. The balance sheet shows:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 100,000 current; 500,000 long-term"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 600,000 as long-term debt"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 500,000 current; 100,000 long-term"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 600,000 as current"},{"t":"a","x":"**7. **A — The portion due within one year is reclassified as current."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **Face 600,000, 6% coupon, interest paid semiannually. Each interest payment is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 36,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 9,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 3,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 18,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**8. **D — 600,000 × 6% × 6/12 = 18,000."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **Which statement about dividends is TRUE?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. A liability is recorded on the date of record"},{"t":"p","x":"B. The liability arises on the declaration date"},{"t":"p","x":"C. No entry is made on the declaration date"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Cash dividends are an expense"},{"t":"a","x":"**9. **B — Declaration creates the liability: Dr Retained earnings, Cr Dividends payable."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **A 2-for-1 stock split:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Needs a journal entry transferring retained earnings"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Doubles shares, halves par, no entry"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Doubles total par value"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Halves the number of shares"},{"t":"a","x":"**10. **B — No entry; par per share halves."},{"t":"p","x":"**11. **Cumulative preferred dividends not declared in prior years are:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Recorded as a liability when they accumulate"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Cancelled"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Reported as an expense"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Disclosed as dividends in arrears"},{"t":"a","x":"**11. **D — Not a liability until declared; disclose."},{"t":"p","x":"**12. **In the indirect method, a loss on the sale of equipment is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Added back in operating activities"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Subtracted from net income in operating activities"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Reported in financing activities"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Ignored, since proceeds are in investing"},{"t":"a","x":"**12. **A — A non-cash loss is added back; the proceeds go to investing."},{"t":"p","x":"**13. **Perpetual weighted average: 10 units @ 10 are in stock, 10 units @ 14 are bought, then 5 are sold. COGS of the sale is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 50"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 70"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 60"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 55"},{"t":"a","x":"**13. **C — Average (100+140)/20 = 12; 5 × 12 = 60."},{"t":"p","x":"**14. **Cash received in advance for gift cards is reported as:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Revenue earned when the cash is received"},{"t":"p","x":"B. A contra-asset reducing receivables"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Stockholders' equity (contributed capital)"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Deferred revenue until redeemed"},{"t":"a","x":"**14. **D — Cash collected but not earned."},{"t":"p","x":"**15. **A company borrows 100,000 at 12% on November 1, interest at maturity. Interest expense at December 31 is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 12,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 1,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 6,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 2,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**15. **D — 100,000 × 12% × 2/12."},{"t":"p","x":"**16. **Not recording accrued interest at year-end causes:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Liabilities overstated, net income understated"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Liabilities understated, net income overstated"},{"t":"p","x":"C. No effect"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Assets understated"},{"t":"a","x":"**16. **B — Expense missing: NI too high, liabilities too low."},{"t":"p","x":"**17. **Compared with issuing shares, issuing bonds:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Dilutes control of existing stockholders"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Creates tax-deductible fixed interest"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Gives bondholders a vote"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Avoids any repayment obligation"},{"t":"a","x":"**17. **B — Interest is deductible and must be paid whether or not there is profit."},{"t":"p","x":"**18. **A patent is acquired for 90,000; legal life 15 years, expected useful life 10 years. Annual amortisation is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 6,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 90,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 15,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 9,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**18. **D — Use the shorter of the economic and the legal life."},{"t":"p","x":"**19. **A mine is bought for 2,400,000 with 1,200,000 tonnes of estimated reserves; 150,000 tonnes are extracted and 120,000 are sold. Depletion included in COGS is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 240,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 300,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 60,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 2,400,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**19. **A — 2 per tonne; only sold tonnes reach COGS."},{"t":"p","x":"**20. **Authorized 2,000,000, issued 1,200,000, treasury 15,000. Shares outstanding are:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 1,185,000"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 1,215,000"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 1,200,000"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 2,000,000"},{"t":"a","x":"**20. **A — Outstanding = issued − treasury."},{"t":"p","x":"**21. **The purchase discount 2/10, n/30 on an 8,000 invoice, paid on day 8 (no returns), saves:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. 80"},{"t":"p","x":"B. 240"},{"t":"p","x":"C. 160"},{"t":"p","x":"D. 320"},{"t":"a","x":"**21. **C — 2% × 8,000 = 160."}]},"BUS2105":{"title":"Mock Final Exam","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Last lesson of the course: a full mock final exam, built from the announced format, the briefing and the Moodle material."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Format"},{"t":"p","x":"Format taken from the Moodle \"Final Assessment Arrangement\" slides: 2 hours, answer THREE questions out of FIVE (each worth about one third of the paper), bring a calculator. Coverage: ALL chapters of the course (1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15), with a balance of sections. Questions are applied, like past papers (case, numbers, diagrams). Draw clearly labelled diagrams wherever you can. Part B is extra drill and is not in the real paper. This mock contains five questions of 33 marks, so you can practise all five, or choose three and time yourself for 2 hours."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"How to work it"},{"t":"li","x":"Open the Exercises tab. Work under exam conditions: closed book, time limit 2 hours, answer three of five questions, calculator, draw clear diagrams."},{"t":"li","x":"Type your answer in the box (or on paper), then tap Show answer to see the model answer and marking guide. Mark yourself Got it or Missed."},{"t":"li","x":"Multiple choice is checked instantly."},{"t":"li","x":"Writing parts: paste your answer in the chat with Claude and ask for a correction against the marks."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Time plan"},{"t":"li","x":"Real paper: 3 questions in 120 minutes, so about 40 minutes per question (33 marks)."},{"t":"li","x":"Practise all five, or choose three and time yourself."},{"t":"li","x":"Draw labelled diagrams on paper next to your typed workings."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part A: Answer three of five (33 marks each)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Demand, supply and elasticity (Chapters 1, 3, 4)"},{"t":"p","x":"The market for egg waffles in a Hong Kong district (quantity in waffles per day, price in HK$ per waffle) has demand **Qd = 600 − 40P** and supply **Qs = −100 + 60P**."},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Find the equilibrium price and quantity, and calculate **total revenue** at equilibrium. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** Egg prices rise and supply becomes **Qs = −220 + 60P**. Find the new equilibrium. Using the **midpoint method**, calculate the price elasticity of demand over the move from the old to the new equilibrium, say whether demand is elastic or inelastic, and what happened to total revenue. (7)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** Explain the **four-case prediction table**: what happens to price and quantity when demand rises and supply rises together, and what happens when demand falls and supply rises? (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** After a viral social-media post, demand rises to **Qd = 760 − 40P** (original supply). Find the new equilibrium. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(e)** Income rises by 10% and demand for egg waffles increases by 4% at every price; the price of a rival snack rises 20% and the quantity of waffles demanded rises 9%. Calculate the income elasticity and the cross elasticity and classify the good and the relationship. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(f)** At the original equilibrium, calculate the price elasticity of demand and of supply with the point formula (|slope| × P/Q). Using your results, explain who bears more of a **per-waffle tax**: buyers or sellers? (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(g)** Chapter 1. Explain **opportunity cost**, **marginal benefit versus marginal cost**, and the role of **incentives**, by using the decision of a student who is thinking whether to open a waffle stall during exam week instead of revising. (5)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(h)** State two factors that make the **demand for waffles** more elastic in the long run than in the short run. (3)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (3)** Qd = Qs: 600 − 40P = −100 + 60P, so 700 = 100P: **P = HK$7, Q = 320**. Total revenue = 7 × 320 = **HK$2,240**.\n**(b) (7)** New equilibrium: 600 − 40P = −220 + 60P, so 820 = 100P: **P = 8.2, Q = 272**. Midpoint method: %ΔQ = (272 − 320) / ((272 + 320)/2) = −48/296 = **−16.2%**; %ΔP = (8.2 − 7) / ((8.2 + 7)/2) = 1.2/7.6 = **+15.8%**. PED = 16.2/15.8 = **1.03**, so demand is (just) **elastic** (about unit elastic). Total revenue falls from 2,240 to 8.2 × 272 = **2,230.4**, consistent with elastic demand (price up, revenue down). Marks: new equilibrium 2; each %Δ 1.5; elasticity 1; classification 0.5; revenue 0.5.\n**(c) (4)** Four-case table: demand up and supply up: quantity **rises** for sure; price change is **ambiguous** (depends on which shift is bigger). Demand down and supply up: price **falls** for sure; quantity ambiguous. (Also: demand up, supply down: price up, quantity ambiguous; demand down, supply down: quantity down, price ambiguous.) Diagram with two shifts for full marks.\n**(d) (3)** 760 − 40P = −100 + 60P, so 860 = 100P: **P = 8.6, Q = 416** (higher price, higher quantity: movement along supply).\n**(e) (4)** Income elasticity = 4% / 10% = **0.4**: positive, so a **normal good**, less than 1, so a **necessity** (inelastic with respect to income). Cross elasticity = 9% / 20% = **0.45**: positive, so the rival snack is a **substitute**.\n**(f) (4)** PED at equilibrium = |−40| × 7/320 = **0.875** (inelastic); PES = 60 × 7/320 = **1.31** (elastic). Since demand is less elastic than supply, **buyers bear a larger share** of the tax (they cannot easily switch; sellers can reduce output).\n**(g) (5)** Opportunity cost = the best alternative forgone (revising: grades, rest); marginal benefit (extra sales, income) versus marginal cost (lost study hours); rational choice compares them at the margin: open the stall for one more hour as long as MB > MC; incentives: higher expected earnings or exam stakes change the choice; also positive vs normative and the \"economist as scientist\" distinction for credit.\n**(h) (3)** More time to find **substitutes** (other snacks), more time to adjust habits; the **larger share of income** spent; broader definition of the good is the opposite (more inelastic). Any two with explanation."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Efficiency, equity and government actions (Chapters 5, 6)"},{"t":"p","x":"The market for rental flats in a city (quantity in thousands of flats, rent R in HK$ thousand per month) has demand **Qd = 100 − 0.5R** and supply **Qs = 10 + R**."},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Find the competitive equilibrium rent and quantity. Explain why this outcome is **efficient**, using marginal benefit, marginal cost, consumer surplus and producer surplus. (5)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** The government imposes a **rent ceiling at HK$40 thousand**. Calculate quantity demanded, quantity supplied, the shortage and the **deadweight loss**. Describe two other costs of the rent ceiling (search activity, black markets, quality) and calculate the **maximum** that a tenant might be willing to pay on the black market for the last flat available. (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** Instead, the government imposes a **tax of HK$9 thousand per flat on landlords**. Find the quantity, the rent paid by tenants, the rent received by landlords net of tax, the tax revenue, the deadweight loss, and the **incidence** on each side. Explain how elasticities determine the incidence. (9)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** Applied to the course debate. A city proposes a **tax on vacant flats**. Explain, with a diagram, the effect on the supply of flats for rent, who is likely to bear the tax, and the efficiency argument for and against such a tax. (5)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(e)** Explain the **big trade-off** between efficiency and equity, the \"fair rules\" versus \"fair results\" views, and use them to evaluate the rent ceiling. Also explain the effects of a **minimum wage** above the equilibrium wage, including who gains and who loses. (6)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (5)** 100 − 0.5R = 10 + R, so 90 = 1.5R: **R = 60, Q = 70**. Efficient: marginal benefit (demand) equals marginal cost (supply) at Q = 70; total surplus is maximised: inverse demand R = 200 − 2Q, inverse supply R = Q − 10; **CS = ½ × 70 × (200 − 60) = 4,900; PS = ½ × 70 × (60 + 10) = 2,450; total surplus 7,350** (HK$ million per month). Any other quantity loses surplus (underproduction: MB > MC; overproduction: MC > MB).\n**(b) (8)** At R = 40: Qd = 100 − 20 = **80**; Qs = 10 + 40 = **50**; shortage **30 thousand** flats; only 50 flats are rented (the short side). **DWL** = ½ × (70 − 50) × (willingness to pay at Q = 50, which is 200 − 100 = 100, minus the marginal cost at Q = 50, which is 40) = ½ × 20 × 60 = **600** (HK$ million per month). Other costs: wasted **search activity** and queues; **black market** or key money; landlords cut quality and maintenance; discrimination; the **maximum a tenant would pay** for the last (50th) flat is **HK$100 thousand per month**, far above the ceiling.\n**(c) (9)** The tax shifts supply up by 9: new inverse supply R = Q − 1; set equal to 200 − 2Q: Q = **67**. Tenants pay R = 200 − 134 = **66** (up 6 from 60); landlords receive 66 − 9 = **57** (down 3 from 60). **Tax revenue = 9 × 67 = 603.** **DWL = ½ × 9 × (70 − 67) = 13.5.** Incidence: buyers 6/9 = **two-thirds**, sellers 3/9 = **one-third**. The tax burden falls mostly on the **less elastic** side: demand is less elastic than supply here (elasticity of demand 0.5 × 60/70 = 0.43 versus supply 1 × 60/70 = 0.86), so tenants bear more. Extreme cases: perfectly inelastic demand means buyers pay all; perfectly elastic supply means buyers pay all.\n**(d) (5)** A tax on vacant flats is a tax on **withholding** flats from the rental market: it **raises** the supply of rental flats (owners prefer to let rather than pay), pushing rents down; owners who cannot let, or who sell, bear part of the tax; owners of empty flats with low opportunity cost respond most. Efficiency argument for: reduces idle resources and shortages, internalises an externality (scarce housing) and raises quantity; against: a tax wedge creates DWL if it distorts the decision (flats unlettable due to repair, transitional vacancy), administrative cost and avoidance (fake lettings); supply of housing is inelastic in the short run, so rents fall little at first. Credit the diagram and the incidence. (Link to your debate position as appropriate.)\n**(e) (6)** **Big trade-off**: policies that redistribute (rent control, minimum wage, progressive taxes) reduce efficiency (DWL, shortages); the \"size of the pie vs how it is shared\". Fairness: **fair rules** (Nozick: voluntary exchange and property rights, \"symmetry of opportunity\") versus **fair results** (utilitarian: transfer from rich to poor increases total utility but incentive costs; Rawls: maximise the position of the worst-off). The rent ceiling is efficient on neither test: reduces quantity supplied; fairness is dubious because it benefits those who find a flat, not necessarily the poor. **Minimum wage** above equilibrium: workers who keep jobs gain; those who lose jobs (employment falls along the demand curve, unemployment arises), and firms lose; DWL and job search; effects depend on elasticity."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Consumer choice (Chapters 8, 9)"},{"t":"p","x":"Mei has **HK$20 per week** to spend on coffee (price HK$4) and buns (price HK$2). Her marginal utilities (in utils) are:"},{"t":"li","x":"Quantity: 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 5th"},{"t":"li","x":"Marginal utility of coffee: 40, 32, 24, 16, 8"},{"t":"li","x":"Marginal utility of buns: 24, 20, 16, 12, 8"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Draw or describe her **budget line** and state the opportunity cost of one coffee. Find the **utility-maximising** combination and the total utility, explaining the rule you use (marginal utility per dollar). (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** The price of coffee falls to **HK$2**. Find the new best choice and total utility, and derive two points of Mei's demand curve for coffee. (6)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** Her income rises to HK$26 (with the original prices). Find her new choice. Explain the difference between a **normal and an inferior good**. (5)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** Chapter 9. Explain what **indifference curves** and the **marginal rate of substitution (MRS)** are, why they are convex, and what the best affordable point satisfies. Using a diagram of a fall in the price of coffee, show the **substitution effect and the income effect**. (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(e)** Explain the **paradox of value** (water and diamonds), and name one finding from **behavioural economics** (for example loss aversion or the endowment effect) that challenges the assumption of rational utility maximisation. (6)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (8)** Budget: 4 × coffee + 2 × buns = 20; maximum 5 coffees or 10 buns; **opportunity cost of one coffee = 2 buns** (slope = −2). Marginal utility per dollar (MU/P):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Unit","1","2","3","4","5"],["Coffee (P = 4)","10","8","6","4","2"],["Buns (P = 2)","12","10","8","6","4"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Buy the units with the highest MU/P until the budget is spent: bun1 (12, $2), coffee1 (10, $4), bun2 (10, $2), coffee2 (8, $4), bun3 (8, $2), coffee3 (6, $4), bun4 (6, $2) = $20. Best choice: **3 coffees and 4 buns**; MU/P is equal (6 = 6) and the budget is exhausted. Total utility = (40 + 32 + 24) + (24 + 20 + 16 + 12) = 96 + 72 = **168** utils. Rule: MU_c/P_c = MU_b/P_b with all income spent.\n**(b) (6)** Coffee at HK$2: MU/P coffee: 20, 16, 12, 8, 4; buns: 12, 10, 8, 6, 4. Best choice: **5 coffees and 5 buns** (cost 10 + 10 = 20); MU/P equal at 4; total utility = 120 + 80 = **200**. Demand curve points: **P = 4, Q = 3; P = 2, Q = 5** (downward sloping).\n**(c) (5)** With income 26 and prices 4 and 2: choose **4 coffees and 5 buns** (cost 16 + 10 = 26; utility 112 + 80 = **192**). Both coffee (3 to 4) and buns (4 to 5) increase, so both are **normal goods**. Normal: demand rises with income; **inferior**: demand falls as income rises (e.g., instant noodles).\n**(d) (8)** **Indifference curve**: combinations giving the same utility; higher curves are preferred; they do not cross. **MRS** = the amount of one good the consumer is willing to give up for one more unit of the other, equal to the slope of the indifference curve; **diminishing MRS** makes the curves convex (people like balance, as one good becomes scarcer, its marginal value rises). Best affordable point: the indifference curve is **tangent** to the budget line: MRS = relative price (P_c/P_b = 2). Fall in the price of coffee: the budget line pivots outwards; total effect from point A to point C; **substitution effect**: move along the original indifference curve to a point with more coffee (relative price changed); **income effect**: move to a higher indifference curve (real income up) which, for a normal good, adds more coffee. Draw a diagram with the intermediate point B on a parallel line tangent to the original curve.\n**(e) (6)** Paradox of value: water has high total utility but low price, as its **marginal** utility is low (abundant); diamonds have low total utility but high marginal utility due to scarcity: price reflects marginal, not total, utility; consumer surplus explains the gap. Behavioural economics (any): **loss aversion** (losses weigh more than gains), the **endowment effect** (value what you own more), **anchoring**, **mental accounting**, **present bias**; they show people use rules of thumb and can be inconsistent, but utility theory still predicts average behaviour."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Costs and perfect competition (Chapters 11, 12)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm in a perfectly competitive market has fixed cost **HK$30** and total variable cost (TVC) as follows:"},{"t":"li","x":"Output (Q): 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6"},{"t":"li","x":"TVC: 0, 20, 36, 48, 64, 90, 126"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a)** Calculate marginal cost (MC), average variable cost (AVC) and average total cost (ATC) for Q = 1 to 6. Explain the shape of the MC curve with **diminishing marginal returns** and say where MC cuts AVC and ATC. (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b)** If the market price is **HK$24**, find the profit-maximising output and the profit. Draw a diagram showing the profit. (5)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c)** Find the **shutdown point** and the **break-even point**. What does the firm do at prices of HK$16 and HK$12? Derive the firm's supply curve. (6)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d)** There are 100 identical firms. Explain the market supply curve and what happens to the number of firms, price and profit in the **long run** when firms in the industry earn economic profit; show the effect of an **increase in demand** in the short run and the long run. (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(e)** Define and give a business example of the **short run versus the long run**; explain what **economies and diseconomies of scale** are and how they shape the long-run average cost curve. Then explain why perfect competition is **efficient**. (6)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (8)**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Q","TVC","MC","AVC","ATC"],["1","20","20","20.0","50.0"],["2","36","16","18.0","33.0"],["3","48","12","16.0","26.0"],["4","64","16","16.0","23.5"],["5","90","26","18.0","24.0"],["6","126","36","21.0","26.0"]]},{"t":"a","x":"ATC = (TVC + 30)/Q. MC first falls (specialisation) then rises beyond Q = 3 because of **diminishing marginal returns**: each extra worker adds less output, so each extra unit costs more. MC cuts AVC at its minimum (around Q = 3 to 4, AVC = 16) and cuts ATC at its minimum (around Q = 4 to 5, ATC ≈ 23.5 to 24): when MC is below the average the average falls, above it the average rises.\n**(b) (5)** Competitive firm: MR = P = 24. Produce where MC = P (MC ≤ 24 up to Q = 4; MC at Q = 5 is 26): **Q = 4**. Revenue 24 × 4 = 96; total cost = 30 + 64 = 94; **profit = HK$2**. Diagram: price line at 24, MC and ATC curves; profit rectangle = (24 − ATC 23.5) × 4 ≈ 2.\n**(c) (6)** **Shutdown point** = minimum AVC = **HK$16** (Q = 3 or 4): below it, revenue does not cover variable cost. **Break-even** (normal profit) = minimum ATC ≈ **HK$23.5** at Q = 4 (zero economic profit). At P = 16: indifferent between producing 3 or 4 units and shutting (loss of 30 = fixed cost in every case); at P = 12: **shut down** (producing 3 units would lose 12×3 − 78 = −42, more than 30). The **supply curve** is the MC curve above minimum AVC: Q = 3 to 4 at about P = 16, Q = 4 for 16 to 26, Q = 5 at 26, Q = 6 at 36; zero below 16.\n**(d) (8)** Market supply = the horizontal sum of the 100 firms' supply (at P = 24, 100 × 4 = 400 units). Short run: firms earn economic profit at price 24 (above min ATC); **new firms enter**, market supply shifts right, price falls to minimum ATC (about 23.5), and **economic profit returns to zero**; if price is below ATC, firms exit. **Increase in demand:** in the short run price rises and existing firms make profit (move up the supply curve); in the long run entry increases supply and price falls back to the original minimum ATC with a larger industry quantity and more firms (constant-cost industry). Diagram: firm and market side by side.\n**(e) (6)** Short run: at least one factor (e.g., the shop's size) is fixed; long run: all factors vary (open a second shop). **Economies of scale**: long-run average cost falls as output rises (specialisation, bulk buying); **diseconomies**: it rises (coordination costs, bureaucracy); the LRAC is the envelope of short-run cost curves; **minimum efficient scale**. **Efficiency of perfect competition**: P = MC = MB so allocative efficiency; zero profit at minimum ATC in the long run so productive efficiency; total surplus is maximised (no DWL)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Monopoly, monopolistic competition and oligopoly (Chapters 13, 14, 15)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a) Monopoly (13).** A monopoly faces the demand **P = 100 − 2Q** and has constant marginal cost **MC = 20** (no fixed cost). (i) Find the marginal revenue curve, the profit-maximising output, the price, and the profit. (ii) Compute what would happen under perfect competition, the **deadweight loss** and the consumer surplus under monopoly. (iii) What happens if the monopolist can practise **perfect price discrimination**? (iv) Explain how a monopoly arises (barriers to entry) and two forms of regulation (average cost pricing, rate of return). (13)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b) Monopolistic competition (9).** A cafe in a district full of cafes earns economic profit now. Explain the short-run decision, what happens in the long run (entry, demand and profit), the long-run equilibrium diagram, **excess capacity**, and why advertising and product differentiation are used. Is monopolistic competition efficient? (9)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c) Oligopoly (11).** Two airlines choose to set a high or low fare. Profits (airline A, airline B) in HK$ million per month are: both high: (4, 4); both low: (2, 2); A low and B high: (6, 0); A high and B low: (0, 6). (i) Find the **Nash equilibrium** and explain the **prisoners' dilemma**. (ii) Explain why the airlines might collude and why the agreement is unstable. (iii) Explain **repeated games** and a **tit-for-tat** strategy. (iv) Explain why price-fixing is illegal under **antitrust or competition law** and the difference between a cartel and tacit collusion. (11)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) Monopoly (13).** (i) Demand P = 100 − 2Q, so **MR = 100 − 4Q**. MR = MC: 100 − 4Q = 20, **Q = 20**; **P = 100 − 40 = 60**; profit = (60 − 20) × 20 = **800**. (4) (ii) Perfect competition: P = MC = 20, **Q = 40**. **Deadweight loss** = ½ × (60 − 20) × (40 − 20) = **400**. Consumer surplus under monopoly = ½ × (100 − 60) × 20 = **400** (versus ½ × 80 × 40 = 1,600 under competition). (4) (iii) With **perfect price discrimination** the monopolist sells 40 units, charging each customer their maximum willingness to pay: profit = ½ × 80 × 40 = **1,600**; consumer surplus 0; **no deadweight loss** (efficient but unfair). (2) (iv) Barriers to entry: **legal** (patents, licences, franchises), **natural** (economies of scale give a natural monopoly), ownership of key resource. Regulation: **average cost pricing** (P = ATC, zero economic profit, some DWL remains), **marginal cost pricing** (efficient but loss, needs subsidy), **rate of return regulation**, price-cap. (3)\n**(b) Monopolistic competition (9).** Short run: the cafe faces a downward-sloping demand (differentiated product), sets MR = MC, charges the price on the demand curve; with price above ATC it earns **economic profit**. Long run: **entry** of new cafes shifts each firm's demand left and makes it more elastic until demand is **tangent to ATC**: **zero economic profit**, price above marginal cost (a markup). **Excess capacity**: output is below the output at minimum ATC. **Efficiency**: not allocatively efficient (P > MC) or productively efficient, but variety and innovation benefit consumers, so the loss may be offset. **Advertising and product differentiation**: create perceived differences and loyalty, signal quality, raise demand and reduce elasticity; but raise costs; brand names guarantee quality. Diagram: tangency point at long-run equilibrium.\n**(c) Oligopoly (11).** (i) For each airline, \"low\" is the best response whatever the rival does (if the rival is high: 6 > 4; if the rival is low: 2 > 0), so the **Nash equilibrium is both low (2, 2)**, although both would prefer (4, 4): the **prisoners' dilemma**. (3) (ii) Airlines can earn more by **colluding** at the high price (monopoly-like outcome 8 total versus 4), but each has an **incentive to cheat** (6 > 4), so the agreement is unstable without enforcement. (3) (iii) **Repeated game**: with repeated interaction, cheating is punished in the future; **tit-for-tat** (cooperate first, then copy the rival's previous move) sustains cooperation when players value the future; the more rounds and the lower the chance of the game ending, the more likely collusion. (3) (iv) **Price-fixing** (an explicit cartel) reduces competition and consumer surplus: illegal under **antitrust and competition law** (e.g., Hong Kong's Competition Ordinance); **tacit collusion** (parallel pricing without an agreement) is harder to prove and legal in many places; cartels' instability and detection (leniency programmes). (2)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part B: Extra drill (15 multiple choice)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **If the price of a substitute rises, the demand for the good:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Decreases"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Stays unchanged"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Increases"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Becomes perfectly inelastic"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **C — A rival's price rise shifts demand for this good right (substitutes)."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **The demand for a necessity with no close substitutes is most likely:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Elastic in the short run"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Inelastic"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Unit elastic"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Perfectly elastic"},{"t":"a","x":"**2. **B — Necessities with no close substitutes: inelastic."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **When demand is elastic, a price cut makes total revenue:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Rise"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Fall"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Stay the same"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Equal zero"},{"t":"a","x":"**3. **A — Elastic demand: %ΔQ exceeds %ΔP, so revenue rises when price falls."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **A price ceiling set above the equilibrium price:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Causes a shortage"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Has no effect"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Causes a surplus"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Raises the equilibrium price"},{"t":"a","x":"**4. **B — A ceiling above equilibrium is not binding."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Deadweight loss arises because:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Producers earn too much"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Some beneficial trades do not occur"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Prices fall"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Taxes are always lower than subsidies"},{"t":"a","x":"**5. **B — Deadweight loss: lost gains from trade."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **The tax burden falls mainly on the side of the market that is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Less elastic"},{"t":"p","x":"B. More elastic"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Larger"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Wealthier"},{"t":"a","x":"**6. **A — The less elastic side cannot easily avoid the tax."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **At the utility-maximising choice:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Marginal utilities are equal across all goods consumed"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Total utilities are equal"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Marginal utility per dollar is equalised"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Prices are equal"},{"t":"a","x":"**7. **C — MU/P equalised across goods."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **A fall in the price of a normal good causes:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. A substitution effect that reduces quantity and an income effect that raises it"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Both effects reduce quantity demanded"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Both effects raise quantity demanded"},{"t":"p","x":"D. No effect"},{"t":"a","x":"**8. **C — For a normal good both the substitution and income effects raise quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **In the short run, a competitive firm's shutdown point is the minimum of:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Average total cost"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Marginal cost"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Average fixed cost"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Average variable cost"},{"t":"a","x":"**9. **D — Shutdown point: minimum AVC."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **In long-run competitive equilibrium, economic profit is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Equal to zero"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Positive"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Negative"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Maximum"},{"t":"a","x":"**10. **A — Entry and exit drive economic profit to zero."},{"t":"p","x":"**11. **A single-price monopolist's marginal revenue is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Equal to price"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Above price"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Equal to marginal cost always"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Below price"},{"t":"a","x":"**11. **D — Single price: to sell more, price falls on all units, so MR < P."},{"t":"p","x":"**12. **Compared with perfect competition, a single-price monopoly produces:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. More at a lower price"},{"t":"p","x":"B. The same at a higher price"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Less at a higher price"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Less at a lower price"},{"t":"a","x":"**12. **C — Monopoly restricts output and raises price."},{"t":"p","x":"**13. **In monopolistic competition in the long run, a firm:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Earns zero economic profit with excess capacity"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Earns economic profit as a monopoly does"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Produces at minimum ATC as under perfect competition"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Is a price taker"},{"t":"a","x":"**13. **A — Zero profit and excess capacity (output below minimum ATC)."},{"t":"p","x":"**14. **In a prisoners' dilemma, the Nash equilibrium is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. The outcome where both players cooperate with each other"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Each player's best choice given the other's"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Always the best joint outcome"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Chosen by a third party"},{"t":"a","x":"**14. **B — Nash equilibrium: each player's best response."},{"t":"p","x":"**15. **Tit-for-tat in a repeated game means:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Always cheating no matter what the rival does"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Always cooperating no matter what the rival does"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Randomising"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Cooperating first, then copying the rival's last move"},{"t":"a","x":"**15. **D — Tit-for-tat: cooperate, then mirror."}]},"BUS2108":{"title":"Mock Final Exam","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Last lesson of the course: a full mock final exam, built from the announced format, the briefing and the Moodle material."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Format"},{"t":"p","x":"Mock paper written from the professor's oral briefing. Time: 2 hours (real paper) · 100 marks · Answer FOUR questions: both questions of Section A, one of Section B, one of Section C. Every question has Part A and Part B and you must answer both parts of the question you pick. Chapters not examined: textbook chapters 5 and 9. Cases below are practice scenarios: figures are illustrative unless stated."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"How to work it"},{"t":"li","x":"Open the Exercises tab. Work under exam conditions: closed book, time limit closed book."},{"t":"li","x":"Type your answer in the box (or on paper), then tap Show answer to see the model answer and marking guide. Mark yourself Got it or Missed."},{"t":"li","x":"Multiple choice is checked instantly."},{"t":"li","x":"Writing parts: paste your answer in the chat with Claude and ask for a correction against the marks."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Time plan"},{"t":"li","x":"Each question has a part A (13 marks) and a part B (12 marks)."},{"t":"li","x":"Define the concept, apply it to the case, then evaluate."},{"t":"li","x":"Keep the figures from the practice-article in your answer."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Section A: Compulsory (Q1)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Chip equipment, trade and market entry (25 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Source extract (practice article, based on widely reported public facts).** \"Europe's chip-equipment champion: how a Dutch firm came to dominate a global industry.\" A company in the Netherlands is the only producer in the world of the most advanced lithography machines used to print the circuits on chips. Each machine costs several hundred million euros, takes years of development, and relies on thousands of components from specialist suppliers across Europe, Asia and the United States. Chipmakers in Taiwan, South Korea and the United States are its customers; no rival has caught up despite large state-funded programmes in several countries. Under pressure from allies, the Dutch government has required export licences for the most advanced machines destined for China since 2023. Industry groups warn that lost sales will reduce the funds available for research, while security officials argue that the technology is too sensitive to sell freely. Analysts add that the firm's lead is partly a historical accident: it grew out of a small research unit of a Dutch electronics group in the 1980s, was later helped by regional subsidies and joint research programmes, and attracted early investment from customers who feared being locked out."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1A. (13 marks)**"},{"t":"p","x":"• (i) Identify the product and the industry described in the article and explain two basic features of the European Union that matter for a firm located in a member state. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (ii) Apply **new trade theory** to the article. Your answer must use economies of scale, first-mover advantage and competitive advantage, and explain how this differs from an explanation based on natural factor endowment (Heckscher-Ohlin). (6)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (iii) Using the article, discuss how governments affect firms in the global business environment. Name at least three instruments of trade policy, say which the article describes, and explain whether the government is acting for political or economic reasons. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1B. (12 marks)**"},{"t":"p","x":"You are now a senior manager at a medium-sized **industrial manufacturer** in the same industry: a European maker of precision components sold to chip-equipment producers."},{"t":"p","x":"• (i) The national government is considering a **voluntary export restraint (VER)** on sales to one large foreign market, negotiated with the importing country. Explain what a VER is, which trade theories and trade policies are relevant, and who gains and loses. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (ii) Weigh the **pros and cons** of such a measure for industrial manufacturers in this industry. Refer explicitly to exports, imports and the **balance of payments**. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (iii) Give **at least four to five recommendations** to your company: where to source suppliers, and which market-entry modes other than exporting (licensing, franchising, joint venture, wholly-owned subsidiary or FDI) it should consider, with reasons. (4)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**Q1A (13)**\n**(i) Product, industry, EU (3).** Product: advanced lithography machines (the equipment that prints circuits on chips). Industry: semiconductor capital equipment, a high-technology industrial-machinery sector that supplies the chip industry. (1)\nEU features (any two, 1 each, applied): a single market with free movement of goods, services, capital and labour, so the firm can source components and sell across 27 members without tariffs; a common external trade policy, so export controls and tariffs towards non-members follow common rules and bargaining happens at EU level (although national governments still license sensitive exports); the euro in 20 member states removes exchange-rate risk inside the euro area; EU competition and state-aid rules limit the subsidies a national government can give. (2)\n**(ii) New trade theory (6).**\n• Definition (1): Krugman's theory that in industries with economies of scale and first-mover advantages, the countries that dominate trade are largely those whose firms happened to start first; also called strategic trade theory.\n• **Economies of scale (1.5):** development of each machine costs billions and the market is small (a few chipmakers), so unit cost falls steeply with volume and learning. World demand supports only one or two producers, so a second entrant would have to build capacity it cannot fill. The article's \"no rival has caught up\" is the result.\n• **First-mover advantage (1.5):** the firm started in the 1980s, accumulated knowledge, and built a web of specialist suppliers and customer co-development. Customers invested early in the firm because they feared being locked out, which reinforces the lead. Barriers to entry: patents, the supplier network, accumulated know-how and the cost of replicating it.\n• **Competitive advantage (1):** the firm's advantage is technological and organisational, created through R&D and its ecosystem, not inherited.\n• **Contrast with natural factor endowment (1):** Heckscher-Ohlin says countries export goods that use their abundant factors (land, labour, capital). The Netherlands has no special endowment in chips; neither does it have unusually cheap labour or raw materials. Its dominance is explained by history, scale and chance. The article itself says the lead is partly \"a historical accident\", which is exactly Krugman's point. Bonus insight: new trade theory also gives a rationale for government support (regional subsidies, joint research programmes) in such industries.\n**(iii) Government and firms (4).** Instruments (any three, 0.5 each plus application): subsidies (the article: regional subsidies and joint research programmes); export controls and licensing, a form of administrative policy (article: licences for the most advanced machines to China since 2023); tariffs; quotas and VERs; local content rules; antidumping duties. (2) Reasons: **political** (national security, pressure from allies, foreign policy) dominate the licence decision; **economic** (protecting a strategic industry and its high-wage jobs, first-mover advantage) explain the earlier subsidies, and the industry's warning shows the cost: lost sales cut R&D funds. Credit for saying governments both help (subsidy, research) and constrain (export licence) firms. (2)\n**Q1B (12)**\n**(i) VER (4).** A VER is a quota on trade that a country imposes on its own exports, typically at the request of the importing country's government, which is why it is \"voluntary\"; the exporter accepts it to avoid worse measures such as a tariff or a quota. Example: Japanese car exports to the United States in the 1980s. (1) Theories and policies relevant: the **mercantilist** logic (protect domestic producers, avoid imports); **new trade theory and strategic trade policy** (protect a strategic industry); against it, **comparative advantage and free trade** (restricting exports reduces gains from trade). Policy family: quota/administrative control, closely related to export controls. (1.5) Gains and losses: gainers: the importing country's domestic producers (less foreign competition), the importing country's government (security), possibly the exporters who stay (higher prices and scarcity rents, since the quota creates \"quota rents\"). Losers: consumers and customers in the importing country (higher prices, less choice), exporting firms that lose sales, workers of the exporting industry, and the long-run technology race (less revenue for R&D). (1.5)\n**(ii) Pros and cons for industrial manufacturers, with trade balance and BoP (4).**\nPros (2): possible higher unit margins on the restricted volume; avoids a heavier tariff or full ban; political goodwill with home and allied governments; protects intellectual property and security-sensitive know-how; stable, predictable regulatory regime.\nCons (2): lost export revenue and scale economies in a scale-driven industry; competitors in non-restricted countries gain share, so permanent loss of the market; customers may redevelop supply locally with domestic or other suppliers; supply-chain costs rise; risk of retaliation (e.g., restrictions on critical inputs such as rare earths).\nTrade and BoP: exports fall, so the goods balance in the **current account** worsens; if the country has a surplus this narrows it, if a deficit it widens it; the firm's foreign income (current account) falls. Imports of inputs may rise if firms shift to foreign suppliers. The BoP always sums to zero through the financial account, so lower exports are offset by changes in capital flows or reserves. Fewer sales to the restricted market may also lead firms to shift production through FDI, which shows up as a financial-account outflow.\n**(iii) Recommendations (4: one mark per developed recommendation, maximum four, 5 for completeness).** Examples:\n(1) **Diversify customers** across non-restricted markets (Taiwan, Korea, United States, Japan, India) to reduce dependence on the restricted market.\n(2) **Dual-source suppliers** and keep critical components from at least two countries to avoid single points of failure; prefer allied-country suppliers for sensitive parts.\n(3) **Move up the value chain**: services, maintenance and upgrades are not covered by export limits on machines and are less scale-sensitive; keep a service contract business.\n(4) **Market entry**: **licensing** for older-generation components (low risk, but loses control and creates a future competitor); **joint venture** with a local partner in a permitted market (shared risk, access, but know-how leakage); **wholly-owned subsidiary or FDI** to serve a major market from inside (Dunning's OLI: ownership advantage = technology; location advantage = proximity to customers; internalisation = avoid leakage); **exporting** only for non-sensitive items.\n(5) **Lobby and comply**: engage with government on clear licensing criteria, build a compliance function, plan for scenario changes (stress-test revenue under a full ban).\n(6) **Hedge currency risk** and keep production costs in euros aligned with sales currency."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Section B: Choose one (Q2 or Q3)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Command economy FDI and deglobalisation (25 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2A. Command political economy (13 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"Your firm, a consumer-electronics assembler headquartered in Hong Kong, is offered a joint venture in a country with a **command form of political economy** (totalitarian, single-party, state-planned). The previous example in the region, a jointly run industrial zone near a heavily militarised border, was shut down by one government after a political dispute. Explain the **difficulties** your firm would face if it made FDI there. Organise your answer around three aspects: (1) the high-level political system, (2) centralisation of the economy, including supply chain, logistics, transport and even product quantities, and (3) the monetary system. Conclude with a recommendation: invest, or not, and under what safeguards?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2B. Deglobalisation (12 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"Commentators speak of slowbalisation or deglobalisation. **Put yourself in the position of a president who campaigns on \"bringing production home\".** Evaluate the **disadvantages of globalisation**, which are the arguments for deglobalisation. Use course concepts such as GDP, purchasing power, and the benefits and costs of trade. Explain the main reasons for the trend (protectionism, reluctance to rely on foreign supply chains, national security, poor relations between countries). Finish by saying which arguments you find weakest and why."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**Q2A command economy (13)**\n**Structure.** Brief definition (1): command political economy: totalitarian control, state planning, state ownership; example North Korea. Then three aspects (3 each), recommendation (3).\n(1) **Political system (3):** total state control, no independent courts, rule by decree, personal relationships with officials decide outcomes, no rule of law, so contracts and property rights are unenforceable; arbitrary seizure or closure (the industrial-zone example: a political dispute shut a zone overnight); sanctions risk from the home government and third countries (UN/US sanctions), reputational risk with consumers and investors in other markets (ethics, human rights, forced labour); political risk is non-insurable in practice; repatriation of profits uncertain; choice of staff and visas controlled by the state.\n(2) **Centralisation (3):** the government controls supply chain, logistics, transport, input allocation and even product quantities: the firm cannot choose suppliers or scale output; no market prices, so cost accounting is distorted; inputs are rationed and quality inconsistent; labour is allocated by the state, wages paid to the state; the firm cannot respond to demand; shipping and ports depend on state permission; the firm cannot sell domestically at market price; strict control of information flows.\n(3) **Monetary system (3):** the currency is not freely convertible, the exchange rate is set by the state (official versus black-market rates), so profits cannot easily be converted and repatriated; wages and local costs are paid in an overvalued local currency; transfer pricing is scrutinised; no access to normal banking or SWIFT for sanctioned states; inflation and arbitrary revaluation risk; so the firm bears hidden costs.\n**Recommendation (3):** best answer: do not invest directly, or invest only after a staged approach: (a) start with low-capital exposure (contract manufacturing/licensing/export) rather than a wholly-owned plant; (b) JV with exit clauses, escrow of profits outside the country, political-risk insurance and arbitration in a neutral venue; (c) legal review of sanctions; (d) set an exit trigger. Also note: Hill's view is that attractiveness depends on benefits (large market, cheap labour), costs and risks. Here costs and risks dominate. Credit any consistent recommendation with the three safeguards.\n**Q2B Deglobalisation (12)**\n**Framing (1):** slowbalisation means trade growth slowing relative to GDP; deglobalisation means actively reducing cross-border integration. From the president's perspective, the aim is to argue the **disadvantages of globalisation**.\n**Arguments (use GDP, purchasing power; 7):**\n• Jobs and wages: import competition hits manufacturing communities; Samuelson's point: for a rich country trading with a poor one, job losses can outweigh cheaper goods; distribution: gains are broad but small, losses concentrated, so a political backlash.\n• GDP and purchasing power: trade raises GDP and consumers' purchasing power through cheaper goods, but if factories close the wage income fall can offset it in affected regions; GDP growth does not measure distribution; a trade deficit subtracts from GDP (net exports) in accounting terms.\n• Dependence and supply chains: pandemic shortages, chips and medical supplies; single-source dependence on one country.\n• National security: critical technology, food, energy; sensitive exports (link to Q1).\n• Poor relations between countries: tariffs, sanctions and geopolitical rivalry (US-China) make integration risky; firms \"friend-shore\".\n• Sovereignty and standards: fears of a race to the bottom on labour and environment; loss of policy control (WTO rules).\n• Environment: long supply chains raise emissions.\n**Reasons for the trend (2):** protectionism; reluctance to depend on foreign supply chains; national security; poor relations; plus technology (automation, 3D printing, reshoring), rising Chinese wages, and the shift from goods to services.\n**Evaluation (2):** the weakest arguments: that protection raises overall welfare (consumers pay higher prices, retaliation hurts exporters, jobs saved are costly: cost per job protected exceeds the wage); that a country can be self-sufficient (comparative advantage). Strongest: security of critical goods and adjustment costs for displaced workers (best solved by retraining, not tariffs). Conclude: deglobalisation is selective (\"de-risking\") rather than total."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Porter's diamond and comparative advantage (25 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3A. Porter's diamond (13 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"Choose **one industry of your own** (not the one used in the lecture example) in one country, for example: Hong Kong financial services, South Korean cosmetics and beauty-medical tourism, Danish wind turbines, Taiwanese contract chipmaking, Swiss watches, or a local industry from your home city. Explain why that country is internationally competitive in that industry using **all six elements** of Porter's diamond: factor conditions, demand conditions, related and supporting industries, firm strategy, structure and rivalry, government policy and chance. **Highlight demand conditions** as the question's focus, but the answer must show how the six interact. Close with one weakness of Porter's theory."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3B. Comparative advantage (12 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"South Korea and Vietnam each use only labour to produce smartphones and rice. Hours of labour needed per unit:"},{"t":"li","x":"South Korea — 1 smartphone: 2 · 1 tonne of rice: 4 · Total labour hours available: 120"},{"t":"li","x":"Vietnam — 1 smartphone: 6 · 1 tonne of rice: 5 · Total labour hours available: 300"},{"t":"p","x":"Assume each country currently splits its hours **equally** between the two goods, and that the two countries can trade at **1 smartphone for 1 tonne of rice**."},{"t":"p","x":"• (i) Which country has an absolute advantage in which good? (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (ii) Calculate the opportunity cost of each good in each country, and state who has the comparative advantage in each. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (iii) Calculate the output of each country before trade and the world total. (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (iv) If each country specialises completely, what is the world output? How many smartphones should Korea exchange for how much rice, and what does each country end up consuming compared with before? (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (v) State two assumptions behind the result and say what changes if transport costs are high. (2)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**Q3A Porter's diamond (13)**\n**Model answer: South Korean cosmetic surgery and beauty-medical tourism** (the professor's suggestion). Structure: definition and diagram (1), six elements (1.5 each = 9), interaction (2), weakness (1).\n(1) **Factor conditions:** advanced factors: a large number of highly trained surgeons and dermatologists, strong clinical research, a dense cluster in one district of Seoul; also infrastructure: international airport, hospitals, translation services. Basic factors are less important: Korea has no natural endowment in this industry. Porter: factors are created, not inherited.\n(2) **Demand conditions (focus):** a sophisticated, appearance-conscious, highly demanding domestic customer base (beauty standards, social-media culture, K-pop and K-drama) forces clinics to innovate, compete on results and customer service; high home demand supplies scale and learning; foreign patients, drawn by the home market's reputation (China, Japan, Southeast Asia, Hong Kong), then reinforce demand; demanding customers set high standards that become export advantages.\n(3) **Related and supporting industries:** cosmetics and skincare (K-beauty), medical devices and lasers, travel agencies and medical brokers, hotels, translation and interpreter services, media and entertainment that create the demand, financial and insurance services.\n(4) **Firm strategy, structure and rivalry:** intense domestic rivalry among hundreds of clinics in the same district (Gangnam), forcing price, service and technology competition; many are private specialist clinics; branding by specialisation; aggressive digital marketing.\n(5) **Government policy:** supports medical tourism (visa rules, promotion, international patient registration, quality accreditation); regulatory framework; but government can also constrain (advertising and safety rules, 2020s regulation on brokers) so Porter says policy influences but does not create advantage.\n(6) **Chance:** the global spread of K-pop and K-drama (the \"Korean wave\"), the 1997 Asian financial crisis which pushed clinics to seek foreign patients, COVID-19 which disrupted travel, exchange-rate shifts.\n**Interaction (2):** rivalry sharpens quality, which attracts demanding customers, which funds supporting industries, which supports exports; the diamond is a self-reinforcing system, and clusters form (geographic concentration). **Weakness (1):** limited empirical testing; hard to measure; ignores multinational firms (an industry can use the diamond of another country), the role of luck is hard to predict; overlaps with new trade theory.\nOther acceptable industries: Hong Kong financial services (strong factors: rule of law, free capital flows; demand: China's financing needs; related: law, accounting, IT; rivalry: dozens of banks; government: low tax, linked rate; chance: 1997 handover), Danish wind turbines, Taiwanese foundry. **Marking:** zero credit for simply reciting the Canada example; deduct if fewer than six elements are analysed.\n**Q3B Comparative advantage (12)**\n**(i) Absolute advantage (2):** Korea needs fewer hours for both phones (2 vs 6) and rice (4 vs 5), so Korea has absolute advantage in both goods. Vietnam has none.\n**(ii) Opportunity costs (3):**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","1 phone costs (in rice)","1 tonne rice costs (in phones)"],["Korea","2/4 = 0.5 t","4/2 = 2 phones"],["Vietnam","6/5 = 1.2 t","5/6 = 0.83 phones"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Korea has the lower opportunity cost of phones (0.5 < 1.2), so it has the comparative advantage in smartphones. Vietnam has the lower opportunity cost of rice (0.83 < 2), so it has the comparative advantage in rice.\n**(iii) Pre-trade output (2):** Korea: 60 h on phones = 30 phones; 60 h on rice = 15 t. Vietnam: 150 h on phones = 25 phones; 150 h on rice = 30 t. World total: 55 phones and 45 t of rice.\n**(iv) Specialisation (3):** Korea: 120/2 = 60 phones. Vietnam: 300/5 = 60 t of rice. World output: 60 phones and 60 t rice, i.e., +5 phones and +15 t. At the exchange rate 1:1 (between 0.5 and 1.2 for phones, so both gain), Korea exports 25 phones for 25 t of rice. Korea consumes 35 phones and 25 t (before: 30 and 15: gains of 5 phones and 10 t). Vietnam consumes 25 phones and 35 t (before: 25 and 30: gains 5 t, no loss in phones). Both countries gain, so trade is a positive-sum game. (Accept any exchange rate strictly between 0.5 and 1.2 t per phone; award full marks for consistent calculation.)\n**(v) Assumptions (2):** (1) two countries, two goods; (2) zero transport costs; (3) constant returns to scale; (4) resources mobile between industries; (5) fixed resources; (6) no effect on income distribution. If transport costs are high they eat into the gains: the exchange ratio must stay within the opportunity-cost band plus transport; if costs exceed the gain, trade is not worthwhile. (State the Samuelson caveat for extra credit.)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Section C: Choose one (Q4 or Q5)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Culture, ethics and CSR (25 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4A. Hofstede (13 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"The question names **one dimension: uncertainty avoidance**. You are a business person who has just set up a **factory** in a country that scores very high on this dimension (for example Greece, which scores 100 in Hofstede's table) and your head office is in a very low-score country (for example Singapore, which scores 8, or Hong Kong). Explain how this dimension affects your company's **policies, management style and procedures** (hiring, contracts, rules, training, performance review, innovation, change). Give at least five concrete examples, mention one criticism of Hofstede's model, and say how your answer would differ for **power distance** or **individualism-collectivism** if the examiner had chosen those instead."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4B. Universalism and relativism (12 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"Your company's code of ethics bans gifts above a modest value and bans any payments to speed up official approvals. In the host country, small gifts and \"speed money\" are a normal part of doing business and local competitors use them freely."},{"t":"p","x":"• (i) Explain **cultural universalism** and **cultural relativism**, with one example each from international business. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (ii) Say which approach each of these would lead to for your company. (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (iii) Explain how a **code of ethics** and **CSR** help a multinational manage this situation, and recommend a policy. Use the three-question ethical decision test and at least three of the seven managerial actions. (6)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**Q4A Uncertainty avoidance (13)**\n**Definition (2):** the extent to which people feel threatened by ambiguous or unknown situations; high score means a need for rules, structure, predictability, formal procedures and security. Greece 100, Singapore 8; Hong Kong also low (flexible workforce, nimble organisations).\n**Applications (8; at least five, 1.5 each):**\n(1) **Policies and rules:** write detailed job descriptions, SOPs, safety manuals and reporting lines; local staff expect clarity on duties, authority and career path; head office's \"figure it out\" style will cause stress.\n(2) **Management style:** managers are expected to have the answer; a boss saying \"I don't know\" loses credibility, unlike in a low-UAI culture; give clear instructions and avoid vague delegation.\n(3) **Hiring and retention:** employees prefer long-term, stable contracts, seniority-based promotion, and are loyal; job-hopping low; offer security, benefits and clear career ladders; probation periods feel threatening, so explain them.\n(4) **Change management and innovation:** resistance to change; introduce new systems gradually with pilots, training, consultation and explicit timelines; innovation proposals need formal channels; risk-taking is not rewarded; set up a structured suggestion scheme.\n(5) **Planning, contracts and decision-making:** detailed contracts and long planning cycles; slower decisions; more meetings, written confirmation and more formal documents (compliance, audits); budget more time for approvals.\n(6) **Performance and communication:** formal appraisal with clear criteria; less tolerance for ambiguity in targets; may need more emotional expression: do not mistake it for conflict.\n(7) **Quality and safety:** strong compliance culture helps (manufacturing quality, safety), a strength to exploit.\n**Criticism (1):** IBM sample, one company; nation=culture assumption; static; mostly male white-collar respondents; dated.\n**Contrast (2):** power distance: high PD means centralised decisions, managers decide, status symbols and top-down communication; individualism-collectivism: collectivist means group incentives, loyalty, indirect communication, harmony, hire through networks. (1 each.)\n**Q4B Universalism and relativism (12)**\n**(i) Definitions with examples (4).** Universalism: values and ethics can be judged by universal standards and a company applies the same standards in every country (example: a firm banning bribes everywhere, bound by the US FCPA/UK Bribery Act; Shell's no-smoking rule everywhere). Relativism: values must be understood in their own cultural context and the firm adapts to local ethical standards (example: HSBC Vietnam closing at lunch/nap time; adapting gift customs). Note: relativism does not mean accepting everything; Hill's \"righteous moralist/naive immoralist\" critique.\n**(ii) Which approach (2):** the code (no gifts above a modest value, no speed payments) is **universalist**; following local competitors' gift giving and speed money would be **relativist** (and potentially illegal, since many home countries criminalise bribery of foreign officials).\n**(iii) Code of ethics, CSR and policy (6).**\n• A code of ethics (also called conduct code) sets universal minimum standards, so employees know what is acceptable, protects against legal liability and reputation damage (2).\n• CSR: accountability to all stakeholders; shows the firm gives back, builds long-term trust with the host community, and reduces reputational risk; CSR is long-term, usually low-profile (1).\n• Three-question test: (i) is it within the code and accepted values? (ii) would I be comfortable if it were published? (iii) would people I respect approve? Speed money fails (i) and (ii) (1).\n• Managerial actions (any three, see Section 5.3): hiring and promotion with ethics criteria; leaders model the behaviour and culture; build ethical criteria into decisions; an ethics officer or ombudsperson; moral courage (walk away from a profitable but unethical deal, support whistle-blowers); CSR as a cornerstone; sustainable strategies (1).\n• **Recommended policy (1):** universal ban on bribes and facilitation payments; a flexible, transparent allowance for small, culturally appropriate gifts (value cap, logged and disclosed, never during bids); train local staff and agents; contract clauses with suppliers; a hotline. This is \"universal core, local adaptation of non-core practices\"."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Corruption and the law of one price (25 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5A. Corruption and the ICAC talk (13 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on the **ICAC talk** you attended, explain the **harms of corruption** in three areas:"},{"t":"p","x":"• (i) which **stakeholder groups** may be harmed; (ii) which **economic systems** may be harmed; and (iii) which **legal systems** may be harmed. The talk may not have covered multinational corporations, so think from the perspective of an **international company and brand** operating in several countries, and explain what a multinational should do to protect itself."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5B. Law of one price (12 marks).**"},{"t":"p","x":"An analyst compiles the following prices for the same standard burger meal. Prices and exchange rates are **illustrative**."},{"t":"li","x":"United States — Local price: US$5.80 · Market exchange rate (local units per US$): n/a"},{"t":"li","x":"Hong Kong — Local price: HK$23.50 · Market exchange rate (local units per US$): 7.80"},{"t":"li","x":"Switzerland — Local price: CHF 6.90 · Market exchange rate (local units per US$): 0.88"},{"t":"li","x":"Japan — Local price: ¥480 · Market exchange rate (local units per US$): 152"},{"t":"p","x":"• (i) State the **law of one price** and show how **arbitrage** is supposed to enforce it. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (ii) For each country other than the United States, calculate the implied PPP exchange rate and say whether the currency is over- or undervalued against the dollar, with the percentage. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (iii) Link the **exchange rate** and **arbitrage** to explain the theory, then explain why the law of one price **may not be feasible in practice** (give at least four reasons), and what this means for a manager planning currency exposure. (5)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**Q5A Corruption (13)**\nStructure: definition (1), three areas (3 × 3 = 9), MNC protection (3). (Adjust to the actual ICAC talk content. The ICAC was established in 1974; its three-pronged strategy is enforcement, prevention and education; the main law is the Prevention of Bribery Ordinance.)\n**Definition (1):** abuse of entrusted power for private gain (bribery, kickbacks, embezzlement, facilitation payments, conflicts of interest, nepotism).\n**Stakeholder groups harmed (3):** shareholders and investors (fines, share-price falls, misallocated capital); employees (unfair hiring and promotion, morale, whistle-blower retaliation); customers and consumers (higher prices, unsafe or poor-quality products because quality checks are bought); honest competitors and SMEs (excluded from tenders); suppliers (paid late or forced to pay kickbacks); governments and taxpayers (lost revenue, wasted public funds); local community (infrastructure collapses, as in poorly built public buildings).\n**Economic systems harmed (3):** resource misallocation (contracts go to the highest briber, not the most efficient); higher costs and lower investment (corruption works as a tax; FDI falls: ties to Chapter 8 and economic growth: Three I's include institutions); distortion of competition and prices; weakens property rights, hence innovation; undermines trust in markets and financial systems; reduces the tax base and growth. Applies to both market economies and mixed or command economies.\n**Legal systems harmed (3):** erodes the rule of law (law applied selectively), weakens courts, police and prosecutors, leads to unenforceable contracts and arbitrary rulings; creates legal risk for MNCs: the **US Foreign Corrupt Practices Act, UK Bribery Act and local laws** have extraterritorial reach, so a bribe paid abroad by a local agent can create liability at home; civil law versus common law systems differ in how contracts and criminal liability are enforced, yet corruption weakens either; harms the integrity of public tenders and licensing.\n**MNC perspective (3):** costs of corruption for MNCs: fines, debarment from tenders, prosecution of executives, loss of brand value and consumer trust, difficulty attracting talent, supply-chain scandals. Protection: a global anti-bribery code and training; due diligence on agents, JV partners and suppliers; third-party audits; whistle-blower hotline; incentive and promotion systems that reward integrity; leaders' tone; refuse \"facilitation\" payments; transparent record-keeping; cooperation with enforcement bodies like the ICAC; CSR and sustainability reporting. In short: corruption harms the brand in all markets, since news travels globally.\n**Q5B Law of one price (12)**\n**(i) (3):** the law of one price: in competitive markets without transport costs and barriers, identical goods must sell for the same price when expressed in a common currency. If not, **arbitrage** (buying where cheap, selling where expensive) pushes prices together. The exchange rate: price in country A = price in country B × exchange rate; the PPP rate is the rate at which prices equalise.\n**(ii) Calculations (4).** PPP rate = local price / US price."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Country","Price in US$ at market rate","Implied PPP rate","Valuation vs US"],["Hong Kong","23.50 / 7.80 = US$3.01","23.50 / 5.80 = 4.05 HK$/US$","**Undervalued by about 48%** (market 7.80 is far above 4.05)"],["Switzerland","6.90 / 0.88 = US$7.84","6.90 / 5.80 = 1.19 CHF/US$","**Overvalued by about 35%** (market 0.88 is below 1.19)"],["Japan","480 / 152 = US$3.16","480 / 5.80 = 82.8 yen/US$","**Undervalued by about 46%** (market 152 above 82.8)"]]},{"t":"a","x":"(1 mark per country, 1 for correct interpretation: an undervalued currency buys more at home than abroad.)\n**(iii) (5).** Link between exchange rate and arbitrage (2): in theory a trader would buy the meal in Hong Kong for US$3.01 and sell it in the US for US$5.80, converting dollars into HK dollars to buy more; buying HK$ raises its value and raising demand in Hong Kong raises its price; the process continues until both prices are equal at the PPP rate, i.e., the exchange rate adjusts (towards 4.05 in the long run), as the lecture says PPP is credible for medium- to long-term movements; relative inflation and the Fisher effect drive adjustments (higher-inflation countries' currencies depreciate). Why not feasible in practice (2, at least four reasons): a burger meal is not tradable (perishable, produced and consumed locally: non-tradable service); transport and transaction costs; tariffs, taxes and regulations (VAT, import duties), product differences; local labour and rent make up much of the price (high-income countries have high wages, so poor countries have structurally lower price levels: the Balassa-Samuelson effect); market power and pricing to market; exchange-rate overshooting and speculation; fixed or managed exchange rates (Hong Kong's peg). The Big Mac index shows currencies that look undervalued do not always appreciate within two years. The professor's view: not feasible. Managerial implication (1): use PPP as a long-run guide only; short-term forecasting is unreliable, so hedge transaction and translation exposure with forwards and lead-lag strategies."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Section D: Extra practice (short answers)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **Explain the difference between GDP per capita and GDP at purchasing power parity and say which better measures living standards."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nGDP per capita at market exchange rates compares incomes converted at market rates; PPP adjusts for local price levels, so it better reflects the goods a person can buy. PPP is the better measure of living standards; neither captures distribution or quality of life (Human Development Index adds education and life expectancy)."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **A country's **balance of payments** shows these transactions: exports of goods US$400 bn; imports of goods US$450 bn; services surplus US$60 bn; a foreign firm buys a US factory for US$30 bn; US residents buy foreign shares for US$25 bn. Classify each as current, capital or financial account and say whether it is a credit or a debit."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nExports of goods US$400 bn: current account, credit (+400). Imports of goods US$450 bn: current account, debit (−450). Services surplus US$60 bn: current account, net credit (+60). A foreign firm buying a US factory: financial account, direct investment inflow (+30, a credit on the liability side, recorded as foreign ownership of US assets). US residents buying foreign shares: financial account, portfolio investment outflow (−25, a debit: net acquisition of foreign assets). Current account balance = 400 − 450 + 60 = +10 bn. Errors and omissions make the total zero."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **What is the difference between a tariff and a quota? Who gains and who loses from each, and why do governments often prefer a tariff?"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nA tariff is a tax on imports; a quota is a limit on quantity. Tariffs: government gains revenue, domestic producers gain, consumers lose through higher prices. Quotas: domestic producers gain, consumers lose, foreign producers with licences gain the scarcity rent, and there is no revenue for the government. Governments often prefer tariffs for revenue and because the effect is transparent and easier to administer."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Explain **Dunning's eclectic (OLI) paradigm** and use it to say why a Hong Kong restaurant chain would open its own restaurants in Singapore rather than license the brand."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nOLI: Ownership advantage (brand, recipes, know-how), Location advantage (a market with strong demand and a good business environment), Internalisation advantage (control quality and protect know-how). Restaurant chain: quality and brand control are essential and licensing risks a partner damaging the brand or copying the model, so internalise through own restaurants (FDI); licensing would be chosen if the market were risky and capital-limited."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Explain translation, transaction and economic exposure with one example each."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nTransaction exposure: a receivable in euros loses value if the euro falls before payment. Translation exposure: a subsidiary's balance sheet in yen is converted into the parent's currency, so reported assets move. Economic exposure: a long-run change in rates alters competitive position (a strong home currency makes exports less competitive)."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **Explain the **impossible trinity** and show which two of its three corners Hong Kong's linked exchange rate system chooses."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nImpossible trinity: a country cannot have a fixed exchange rate, free capital movement and independent monetary policy simultaneously. Hong Kong keeps a fixed (linked) rate and free capital movement and gives up independent monetary policy (interest rates follow the US)."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **Explain the **Leontief paradox** and the **product life cycle theory**, and say which explains better why many electronics assembly jobs moved from the United States to Asia."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nLeontief paradox: the US, capital-abundant, exported labour-intensive goods and imported capital-intensive goods, contradicting Heckscher-Ohlin. Product life cycle (Vernon): products are first made in the innovating rich country, then, as demand matures and standardises, production moves to lower-cost countries. The life cycle explains better why electronics assembly moved to Asia."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **A host country offers a foreign firm tax holidays and subsidies for a plant. List two benefits and two costs for the host country."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nBenefits: jobs, technology and skills transfer, capital inflows, tax revenue and exports. Costs: loss of tax revenue (holidays), subsidies as a fiscal cost, crowding out of local firms, loss of sovereignty, possible dependence and profit repatriation (adverse effect on the host's BoP)."}]},"BUS2202":{"title":"Mock Final Exam","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Last lesson of the course: a full mock final exam, built from the announced format, the briefing and the Moodle material."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Format"},{"t":"p","x":"Format copied from the Moodle exam-revision deck: TIME ALLOWED 2 HOURS. This paper contains FOUR questions and carries 100 marks. Part A (40 marks): attempt the compulsory question. Part B (60 marks): attempt TWO of the three questions (30 marks each). Marks for each part are in brackets. Give labelled diagrams where they make answers clearer. Case-based questions: define the concept briefly, then apply it to the facts. Main chapters for the real paper: Motivation, Team Dynamics, Leadership. Part C is extra drill and not part of the real paper."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"How to work it"},{"t":"li","x":"Open the Exercises tab. Work under exam conditions: closed book, time limit 2 hours."},{"t":"li","x":"Type your answer in the box (or on paper), then tap Show answer to see the model answer and marking guide. Mark yourself Got it or Missed."},{"t":"li","x":"Multiple choice is checked instantly."},{"t":"li","x":"Writing parts: paste your answer in the chat with Claude and ask for a correction against the marks."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Time plan"},{"t":"li","x":"Part A: 40 marks, about 45 minutes (four sub-parts of 10 marks)."},{"t":"li","x":"Part B: two questions of 30 marks, about 30 minutes each."},{"t":"li","x":"Name the theory, apply it to the case facts, then evaluate."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part A (40 marks): compulsory"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Harbour Lights Logistics"},{"t":"p","x":"Harbour Lights Logistics is a Hong Kong freight-forwarding company with 420 staff in Kwai Chung, Shenzhen and Singapore. It was acquired nine months ago by Meridian Cargo, a Rotterdam-based group with a flat structure and a \"speak up\" culture. Harbour Lights was founded by Mr. Lau, who still chairs the board, and is known for strict hierarchy, long hours and loyalty. Since the acquisition:"},{"t":"li","x":"Meridian's new managing director, **Ms. Verhoeven**, has introduced open-plan offices and expects junior staff to challenge decisions in meetings. Most Hong Kong staff stay silent and send their views privately afterwards."},{"t":"li","x":"Operations manager **Daniel** is a conscientious, low-extraversion planner. He believes that the new system \"makes everyone's job less predictable\". He is increasingly irritable and has taken three sick days this month. Colleagues describe him as \"always saying yes in meetings and then doing it his own way\"."},{"t":"li","x":"The Singapore customer-service team smiles through abusive calls from clients; staff report feeling \"empty\" at the end of shifts. Two of the best agents have resigned."},{"t":"li","x":"Sales director **Mei** has the support of Mr. Lau and controls access to the key customer database. Several managers do not challenge her, even when her forecasts are wrong, because \"she can block any budget\"."},{"t":"li","x":"A recent proposal to merge the Shenzhen and Hong Kong dispatch teams has caused arguments between the two team leaders about shifts, and each blames the other for missed deadlines."},{"t":"li","x":"Ms. Verhoeven plans to \"change the culture in 12 months\" by announcing a new vision at a town-hall meeting and replacing the performance appraisal form."},{"t":"p","x":"• **(a) Individual behaviour (10 marks).** Use the MARS model to explain Daniel's behaviour. Identify at least four elements of the model that apply, with evidence from the case. Using the Five-Factor model, say which personality dimensions Daniel appears to be high or low on and what that means for his reaction to the change. (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(b) Emotions and stress (10 marks).** (i) Explain emotional labour and the consequences for the Singapore team, using the concepts of surface acting and deep acting. (ii) Identify the stressors at Harbour Lights and name two stress-management strategies the company should use. (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(c) Power and conflict (10 marks).** (i) Identify the sources of power Mei holds and the contingencies that make her power effective. (5) (ii) Using the conflict process model and the conflict-handling styles, analyse the dispute between the two team leaders and recommend two structural approaches to resolve it. (5)"},{"t":"p","x":"• **(d) Culture and change (10 marks).** (i) Using Lewin's force field analysis, identify two driving forces and three restraining forces at Harbour Lights. (4) (ii) Explain why Ms. Verhoeven's plan is likely to fail, and recommend a better approach using the stages of unfreezing, changing and refreezing, together with at least two strategies for strengthening the new organisational culture. (6) (10 marks in total for (d), including any extra credit for linking to the iceberg model and the merging strategies: assimilation, deculturation, integration, separation.)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) Individual behaviour (10)**\n**MARS model (6):** behaviour and results depend on Motivation, Ability, Role perceptions, Situational factors (MARS); individual characteristics (personality, values, self-concept, perceptions, emotions and attitudes, stress) feed into these.\n• Motivation: Daniel's direction, intensity and persistence of effort fall: he says yes in meetings and then does it his way; irritability and sick days show weak motivation to the new system.\n• Role perceptions: his understanding of what is expected is unclear: Meridian wants challenge, the old culture wanted obedience; \"less predictable\" is a role ambiguity complaint.\n• Situational factors: open plan, new IT system, new hierarchy: constraints that block his planning style.\n• Ability: not the problem if he is competent, but the new system may require new competencies (training needed).\n• Also: values and perception (loyalty to the founder), stress/emotions.\n**Five-Factor (CANOE) (4):** high **conscientiousness** (planner, structured); low **extraversion** (low-extraversion planner, avoids confrontation); likely low **openness to experience** (dislikes change) and higher **neuroticism** (irritable, anxious about unpredictability). Agreeableness: \"yes in meetings\" may reflect cultural politeness, not high agreeableness. Implication: he needs clear plans, training and gradual change; a high-conscientiousness person works well when roles and processes are defined. (Accept MBTI mention: Introvert/Sensing/Judging.)\n**(b) Emotions and stress (10)**\n**(i) Emotional labour (5):** the effort, planning and control needed to express organisationally desired emotions during interpersonal transactions. Surface acting: displaying the required emotion while feeling different (the agents smile through abuse): produces emotional dissonance, exhaustion, burnout, lower job satisfaction, turnover (two best agents resigned). Deep acting: genuinely trying to feel the required emotion (empathising with the customer): less harmful, but demanding. Helpful factors: emotional intelligence of the staff, hiring for it, allowing breaks, empowering agents to end abusive calls, and supportive supervisors.\n**(ii) Stressors and strategies (5):** stressors: role ambiguity and conflict (new expectations), emotional labour, job insecurity after acquisition, work overload and long hours, change itself, interpersonal conflict, lack of control (hierarchy vs challenge culture), workplace violence/abuse by customers. Strategies (any two, with detail): remove the stressor (reduce workload, clarify roles, protect staff from abusive callers); withdraw from stressors (rotation, breaks, holidays, flexible time); change stress perceptions (training, positive framing, self-efficacy); control the consequences (fitness programmes, wellness, employee assistance programme, counselling); receive social support (mentoring, team debriefs).\n**(c) Power and conflict (10)**\n**(i) Sources (5):** Legitimate (her formal position as sales director); reward (she controls budgets); coercive (\"she can block any budget\"); expert (access to and mastery of the key customer database); referent (backed by Mr. Lau and the founder's trust: association with a powerful person). Contingencies: **substitutability** (no one else controls the database: hard to substitute), **centrality** (sales is central to revenue), **discretion** (freedom to decide on budgets), **visibility** (visible to the chairman). Power can distort: managers comply instead of committing, bad forecasts go unchallenged, risk of resistance and politics. Social networks and the chairman's backing amplify her power.\n**(ii) Conflict (5):** process: sources (task interdependence, differentiation, scarce resources, ambiguous rules) → perceived and felt conflict → manifest conflict (blaming) → escalation or resolution. Here: interdependence plus competing shifts and ambiguity about accountability; relationship conflict is replacing task conflict (blame). Style: both leaders use **forcing/win-lose** or avoiding. Recommend: **problem solving** (win-win), and structural approaches (any two): emphasise superordinate goals (joint delivery KPI), reduce differentiation (joint team-building, job rotation), improve communication and mutual understanding, reduce task interdependence (clear handoffs), increase resources (more dispatchers), clarify rules and procedures (shift allocation policy). If unresolved: third-party mediation, arbitration or inquisition by a manager.\n**(d) Culture and change (10)**\n**(i) Force field (4):** driving forces: Meridian's group strategy and standards, competitive pressure, customer expectations, parent company resources. Restraining forces: Hong Kong power distance and hierarchy, founder's loyalty and Mr. Lau's influence, fear of unknown and job insecurity (direct costs), saving face (public challenge), habits and routines, incongruent systems (old appraisal form), Mei's power.\n**(ii) Why the plan fails and a better approach (6):** culture is the deeply embedded iceberg (assumptions and values below the surface; artefacts above it): a speech and a form change only artefacts. No unfreezing (no urgency or participation), no real refreezing (no rewards and symbols that reinforce). It also treats the merger as one-sided (Meridian's culture imposed = **assimilation**); evidence of conflict suggests **deculturation** risk; better **integration** (combine the best of both) or **separation** for a time. Better approach: **unfreezing**: communicate the urgent need, reduce restraining forces by dialogue, involve staff; **changing**: pilot teams, training, role models; **refreezing**: align rewards, appraisal, symbols and promotion with the new norms. Strengthen culture with: actions of founders and leaders (Mr. Lau must visibly support); culturally consistent rewards; maintaining a stable workforce; managing the cultural network (storytellers, heroes); and selecting and **socialising** newcomers (pre-employment, encounter, role management). Strategies to reduce resistance: communication, learning, involvement, stress management, negotiation (coercion last)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part B (60 marks): answer TWO of Questions 2, 3 and 4"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Motivation (30 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Harbour Lights Logistics, continued.** The Shenzhen customs-clearance team handles 150 shipments a day. Managers say the team is \"just working for the pay cheque\". Last year the company introduced an annual bonus based on total company profit (paid in March), plus a monthly \"employee of the month\" award. Six months later, absenteeism has risen. Interviews with staff reveal:"},{"t":"li","x":"Clearance agent **Ivy** says: \"I know how to do the work, but the new software is in Dutch and nobody trained us. Whatever I do, I can't reach the 98% accuracy target.\""},{"t":"li","x":"Agent **Kai** says: \"Even if I reach the target, my supervisor gives the best shifts to his friends. The employee of the month was the supervisor's cousin.\""},{"t":"li","x":"Agent **Rui** says: \"I do not care about the bonus. I would prefer flexible hours so I can look after my mother.\""},{"t":"li","x":"Rui's colleague **Jun** says: \"I earn the same as Ivy but I clear 30% more shipments and I also train the new hires. It is not fair. I am cutting down my effort.\""},{"t":"p","x":"**2a.** Define **intrinsic and extrinsic motivation** (4 marks) and explain how each applies to the Shenzhen employees, using the case (6 marks). (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"**2b.** Using **Expectancy Theory**, explain the three components, and diagnose where Ivy, Kai and Rui each have a problem. Give **two specific strategies** for each component to increase motivation. (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"**2c.** Using **Equity Theory**, describe the theory and explain Jun's situation. Identify the **ways Jun can reduce the inequity tension** and which ones are most harmful for the company. Link to **organisational justice** (distributive, procedural, interactional) in the case of the supervisor and employee of the month. (10)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**2a. (10).** Definitions (4): **intrinsic** motivation: pursuing an activity for its own sake, anchored in innate drives for competence and autonomy; **extrinsic**: motivated by instrumental outcomes (pay, recognition) that indirectly fulfil needs. Application (6): extrinsic: profit-based annual bonus (paid in March: weak link, too remote), employee-of-the-month award (extrinsic, but seen as unfair); intrinsic: Rui wants flexibility (autonomy), Jun enjoys training others (competence and relatedness), Ivy wants to master the system (competence). Managers' view (\"just the pay cheque\") shows extrinsic emphasis; the research says extrinsic rewards rarely destroy intrinsic motivation but badly designed rewards fail to motivate. Recommend job enrichment, autonomy, competence development and a balance of rewards.\n**2b. (10).** Theory (4): motivation = **E-to-P** (effort leads to performance) × **P-to-O** (performance leads to outcomes) × **outcome valences**; multiplicative: any zero kills motivation. Diagnosis (3): **Ivy**: low E-to-P (no training, Dutch software, impossible target). **Kai**: low P-to-O (rewards not linked to performance; favouritism). **Rui**: low valence (bonus not valued; wants flexibility). Strategies (3 marks; at least two per component): E-to-P: train and adjust duties to skills; provide time and resources (translate software); coaching and behavioural modelling to build self-efficacy. P-to-O: measure performance accurately; explain how rewards link to performance; show examples of coworkers rewarded; use transparent criteria. Valence: ensure rewards are valued; individualise rewards (flexi-time, leave); minimise countervalent outcomes (e.g., peers resent high achievers).\n**2c. (10).** Equity theory (4): people compare their outcome/input ratio to a comparison other's; perceived inequity creates tension that motivates restoring balance. Jun (3): inputs higher (30% more clearances plus training), outcomes equal to Ivy: under-rewarded; he chooses to **reduce inputs**. Other options: increase outcomes (ask for a raise, seek promotion), increase others' inputs (ask Ivy to do more), reduce others' outcomes, change perceptions (rationalise), change comparison other, **leave the field** (quit, transfer). Most harmful: reducing inputs (lower productivity) and leaving (losing a trainer and top performer). Justice (3): **distributive** (unfair outcomes: employee-of-the-month to the cousin), **procedural** (no consistent, unbiased criteria, no appeal or voice), **interactional** (supervisor's lack of respect and explanation). Fixes: transparent criteria, objective measurement, employee voice, appeals, explanations."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Team Dynamics (30 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Pacific Pixel Studios.** Pacific Pixel is a 90-person game developer in Kowloon Bay. A new game, \"Typhoon Chase\", is built by a 14-person team made up of eight programmers, three artists, two sound designers and one producer. The team was formed in January from five departments and works on an open-plan floor. The project has run 4 months behind schedule. Observations:"},{"t":"li","x":"Programmers, artists and sound designers sit in three separate clusters and rarely talk, and each blames the others for delays. Two programmers are in their fifties, the rest are in their twenties."},{"t":"li","x":"In weekly meetings the producer, **Sam**, speaks first and the group usually agrees. Younger staff avoid disagreeing. A senior artist, **Priya**, mentioned an idea once and was told \"that is not how we do it here\"."},{"t":"li","x":"The team has a norm of arriving at noon and working until midnight. A new colleague who arrived at 9 a.m. was told to \"fit in\"."},{"t":"li","x":"The producer has no way to see each member's contribution; one programmer, **Leo**, has been seen browsing sports websites during working hours but his output is not checked."},{"t":"li","x":"Meetings are held on three video-call platforms, and 4 members work remotely from Manila and Taipei."},{"t":"li","x":"The team is very close and proud. They have decided to ship the game on time by skipping all testing, saying \"we know our players better than any tester\"."},{"t":"p","x":"**3a.** Using the **team effectiveness model**, evaluate the team's design (task, size, composition) and its organisational environment. Explain what the **levels of task interdependence** are and which applies here. (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"**3b.** Apply the **stages of team development** to the team's history, explain the roles of **team norms and cohesion**, and explain why the decision to skip testing might happen, linking to **team decision-making constraints**. Recommend **four ways to improve team decisions**. (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"**3c.** (i) Identify **social loafing** in the case, say why it occurs and propose four ways to reduce it. (5) (ii) Explain the **advantages and disadvantages of diversity** in this team's composition, including faultlines. (3) (iii) Give two success factors for the **remote team members** and two **creative team structures** (brainstorming, brainwriting, nominal group technique) the producer could use. (2) (10 marks in total.)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**3a. (10).** Model (3): effectiveness depends on the **organisational and team environment** (rewards, communication systems, leadership, structure, physical space), **team design** (task, size, composition) and **team processes** (development, norms, roles, cohesion, trust). Evaluation: **task** (complex, divisible, but producing a game involves high interdependence, so teams are suitable); **size** 14 is large: more process loss, social loafing, slower development: split into sub-squads (typically 5-9); **composition**: diversity of skills (positives) but clusters (faultlines: function and age); lack of \"teamwork\" behaviours (5 Cs). Environment: open-plan space is ok but sound designers' needs differ; no individual measurement or team-based reward; multiple video platforms (communication systems poor); leadership dominated by Sam. Task interdependence (3): **pooled** (independent contributions, e.g., photocopier), **sequential** (assembly line), **reciprocal** (outputs go back and forth, e.g., medical team). The game studio is **reciprocal**: artists, programmers and sound designers continuously adjust each other's work: highest need for coordination and communication.\n**3b. (10).** Stages (2): forming, storming, norming, performing (adjourning); the team seems stuck at **storming** (blame, silos, no shared identity); two underlying processes: team identification and shared mental models and routines. Norms and cohesion (3): norms are informal rules (arrive at noon, \"that's not how we do it\") formed by early experiences and critical events; cohesion (attraction to the team) is high among sub-groups and the whole is \"very close\". **High cohesion with norms misaligned with organisational goals harms performance**: skipping testing. Why skipping testing (3): **overconfidence** (inflated team efficacy: self-enhancement, cohesion, external threat of deadline, mutually reinforcing beliefs), **peer pressure to conform** (Sam leads, younger staff stay silent), **evaluation apprehension** (Priya silenced), **time constraints/production blocking**. Recommendations (2, four needed): checks and balances (rotate chair, devil's advocate), optimum team size, encourage confidence but monitor overconfidence (external QA review), norms of critical thinking, psychological safety, creative structures.\n**3c. (10).** (i) **Social loafing (5):** individual effort falls when contributions are hidden (Leo): causes: hidden performance, low intrinsic motivation, large team, motivation-sapping dynamics. Remedies: smaller teams, specialise tasks, measure individual performance, job enrichment, awareness of obligations, team-based and individual rewards. (ii) **Diversity (3):** advantages: multiple perspectives, broader knowledge base, better representation; disadvantages: slower team development, **faultlines** (age and function splits), more conflict risk. (iii) **Remote teams (2):** success factors: strong use of the \"5 Cs\" teamwork behaviours (coordinate, communicate, cooperate, etc.), a toolkit of channels (not three platforms in conflict), high task structure, face-to-face meetings at times. Creative structures: **brainstorming** (rules: speak freely, do not criticise, many ideas, build on others; limits: production blocking), **brainwriting**, **electronic brainstorming** (low blocking and apprehension), **nominal group technique** (brainwriting plus verbal stage): best for quieter staff like Priya."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Leadership (30 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Everest Bank (Hong Kong).** Ms. Fong became CEO of Everest Bank two years ago. Her style:"},{"t":"li","x":"Delivers an annual \"Bank 2030\" speech about becoming \"the most trusted bank for ordinary families\", using stories and slogans, but she works from a private office and travels first class. Staff say she \"talks about families but never visits branches\"."},{"t":"li","x":"She sets goals and deadlines for each unit, requires weekly reports, and checks work progress personally. Staff appreciate clarity but turnover among young relationship managers is 25% a year."},{"t":"li","x":"Branch managers have little say in decisions. Her deputy, **Mr. Chan**, is warm, listens to employees, organises team lunches and remembers birthdays but is not good at setting priorities."},{"t":"li","x":"A new group of data scientists is highly trained, experienced and self-managed. Ms. Fong's close monitoring annoys them."},{"t":"li","x":"In an interview, Ms. Fong says: \"I am who I am. I do not need feedback.\""},{"t":"li","x":"The press describe her as \"a visionary\" and credit her for the bank's 8% profit growth, although most of the growth followed a rise in market interest rates."},{"t":"li","x":"Two board members worry that she is \"intoxicated by her own image\"."},{"t":"p","x":"**4a.** Identify **task-oriented and people-oriented** behaviours in the case and evaluate which needs further development. Explain the **positive effects** of both styles and how they can be practised effectively. (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"**4b.** Evaluate Ms. Fong using **transformational leadership**: which of the four elements are present, which are missing, and how does this differ from **charismatic leadership**? Link to **managerial leadership** and **servant leadership** (Mr. Chan). (10)"},{"t":"p","x":"**4c.** Use the **Path-Goal model** to recommend a style for (i) the new relationship managers and (ii) the data scientists, and explain **leadership substitutes**. Then discuss the **implicit leadership** problem in the press coverage (romance of leadership), **authentic leadership**, and the role of the **dark triad** if Ms. Fong's confidence became narcissism. (10)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**4a. (10).** Task-oriented behaviours: assign work, set goals and deadlines, clarify responsibility, provide feedback, plan, establish procedures (Ms. Fong: goals, deadlines, weekly reports). People-oriented: concern for employee needs, pleasant workplace, recognise contributions, listen (Mr. Chan). Evaluation: Ms. Fong is heavily **task**-oriented and lacks **people-oriented** behaviour (turnover, no visits, no listening); Mr. Chan has the opposite. Both are needed; both have different effects: task: clarity, performance; people: satisfaction, retention. Recommend: Ms. Fong adds listening, recognition and branch visits; Mr. Chan sets priorities; practise both situationally; the two together is \"shared leadership\" potential. **Shared leadership** (if the question includes it): leadership as a role, flourishes when formal leaders delegate power, culture is collaborative, and employees have influence skills.\n**4b. (10).** Transformational elements (4): (1) develop and communicate a strategic vision: present (Bank 2030, stories), though abstract; (2) model the vision: **missing** (first class, private office, no branch visits); (3) encourage experimentation: **missing** (close monitoring, little say); (4) build commitment: weak (no empowerment). Charisma vs transformational (3): charisma is a personal trait producing referent power and dependent followers; transformational is a set of behaviours engaging followers through trust, empowering them; risk of \"intoxication\" by one's own charisma. Managerial (1.5): \"doing things right\" with stable focus: Ms. Fong's weekly reports are managerial; both are interdependent. Servant (1.5): Mr. Chan is closer to servant leadership (serves followers' needs, humble, empathetic, ethical), essentially a variant of people-oriented leadership.\n**4c. (10).** Path-goal (3): leaders clarify P-to-O expectancies and goals using four styles: directive, supportive, participative, achievement-oriented. (i) New relationship managers (young, unstructured task, stressful): **supportive** plus **directive** at the start (clarity, coaching), then participative; (ii) Data scientists (high ability, self-managed): **participative** and **achievement-oriented**; directive would annoy. Substitutes (2): training, experience and a cohesive team can replace task-oriented or supportive leadership; but do not fully replace leadership. Implicit leadership (2): leadership prototypes and the **romance of leadership**: the press credit her for growth that came from interest rates; we inflate leaders' effects because of the need to simplify and for a sense of control. Authentic (1.5): know yourself (seek feedback) and be yourself: her \"I do not need feedback\" contradicts self-awareness. Dark triad (1.5): narcissism, Machiavellianism, psychopathy: a narcissistic leader pursues a vision for self-glorification, uses unethical influence tactics, worse team outcomes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part C: Extra drill (30 multiple choice)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **A bank pays staff bonuses only on product sales, and employees start mis-selling products. Which MARS element does the bonus mainly target?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Ability"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Role perceptions"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Situational factors"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Motivation"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **D — Pay-for-sales channels the direction and intensity of effort: motivation."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **Lena has a clear, achievable target, but she believes that extra effort will not change her appraisal rating at all. Which expectancy component is weakest?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. E-to-P expectancy"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Outcome valence"},{"t":"p","x":"C. P-to-O expectancy"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Self-efficacy"},{"t":"a","x":"**2. **C — Performance does not lead to the outcome: low P-to-O."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Which statement about four-drive theory is FALSE?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Drives are innate and shared by all people everywhere"},{"t":"p","x":"B. The drive to bond supports collaboration and teamwork"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Needs are goal-directed forces shaped by norms and experience"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Drives form a ladder, satisfied from the lowest up"},{"t":"a","x":"**3. **D — No fixed hierarchy in four-drive theory (that is Maslow)."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Rahul learns that a peer doing the same job is paid more and starts arriving late. In equity theory terms he is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Changing his comparison other"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Reducing his own inputs"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Leaving the field"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Increasing his outcomes"},{"t":"a","x":"**4. **B — Arriving late reduces inputs to restore the ratio."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **A supervisor refuses a bonus but explains the reasons honestly and treats the employee with respect. This mainly shows:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Distributive justice of the outcome"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Procedural justice of the process"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Interactional justice"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Equity restoration by the firm"},{"t":"a","x":"**5. **C — Respect and explanation are interactional justice."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **McClelland: an employee with a high need for achievement will most prefer:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Moderate-risk tasks with feedback"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Very risky tasks that promise very large bonuses"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Easy tasks that the whole team will approve of"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Tasks that give control over other people"},{"t":"a","x":"**6. **A — nAch: moderate-risk goals, feedback."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **Which is an example of positive reinforcement in OB Mod?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Dropping a monthly audit once targets are met"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Cutting pay after late arrivals every week"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Praise after a good customer call"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Ignoring complaints until the complaints stop"},{"t":"a","x":"**7. **C — A pleasant consequence is added after the desired behaviour; A is negative reinforcement."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **A firm tells its staff to 'do your best'. Which characteristic of effective goals is most obviously missing?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Review of progress with the team"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Time frame for completion"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Relevance to the employee's control"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Specificity"},{"t":"a","x":"**8. **D — 'Do your best' is not specific."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **In strengths-based coaching, the first step is for the employee to:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. List the weaknesses to fix"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Identify an area of strength"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Compare themselves with the average"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Sign a disciplinary plan"},{"t":"a","x":"**9. **B — Start from the employee's strength."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **Four engineers complete one design where the output of each becomes the input of the next. Task interdependence is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Sequential"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Pooled"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Reciprocal"},{"t":"p","x":"D. None"},{"t":"a","x":"**10. **A — One-way flow = sequential."},{"t":"p","x":"**11. **Which is NOT a recommended way to reduce social loafing?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Form smaller teams where possible"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Measure individual performance regularly"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Specialise tasks so roles differ"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Reward only the team total"},{"t":"a","x":"**11. **D — Hidden contributions encourage loafing."},{"t":"p","x":"**12. **A new team is polite, unsure of roles and avoids conflict. This is the stage of:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Storming"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Norming"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Forming"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Performing"},{"t":"a","x":"**12. **C — Forming."},{"t":"p","x":"**13. **A very cohesive team has a norm of working slowly so nobody looks bad. Performance will probably:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Rise, because cohesion always helps performance"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Fall, as the norm opposes company goals"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Be unaffected by cohesion"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Rise as long as the team is large"},{"t":"a","x":"**13. **B — Cohesion amplifies the norm; if misaligned, performance drops."},{"t":"p","x":"**14. **Swift trust is best described as:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Trust earned only after years of working together"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Trust based on shared identity and mutual understanding"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Trust based on consistent rewards and punishments"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Initial trust given because someone joined"},{"t":"a","x":"**14. **D — Definition of swift trust."},{"t":"p","x":"**15. **Production blocking in brainstorming happens because:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Members fear being judged by others"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Members copy each other's ideas"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Only one person can speak at once"},{"t":"p","x":"D. The facilitator is highly skilled at managing"},{"t":"a","x":"**15. **C — Only one speaker at a time."},{"t":"p","x":"**16. **Which technique combines brainwriting with a verbal discussion stage?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Nominal group technique"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Electronic brainstorming"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Devil's advocate"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Delphi method"},{"t":"a","x":"**16. **A — Nominal group technique."},{"t":"p","x":"**17. **A self-directed team is most likely to succeed when it:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Has little interdependence among its own members"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Depends heavily on other teams to get work done"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Has no clearly defined task so it can improvise"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Owns a whole work process"},{"t":"a","x":"**17. **D — Whole work process plus autonomy."},{"t":"p","x":"**18. **Transformational leadership differs from charisma mainly because it:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Relies on referent power and personal magnetism"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Empowers followers"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Is mainly a personal trait that people either have or lack"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Avoids communicating a vision altogether"},{"t":"a","x":"**18. **B — Behaviours that empower vs trait that creates dependent followers."},{"t":"p","x":"**19. **A CEO preaches thrift but flies a private jet on short trips. Which transformational element is she failing?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Communicating a vision"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Encouraging experimentation"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Modelling the vision"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Building the vision's rewards"},{"t":"a","x":"**19. **C — Not walking the talk."},{"t":"p","x":"**20. **Managerial leadership is best described as:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Doing the right things in a dynamic setting through a new vision"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Daily guidance and support that sustain current objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Building an aspirational higher purpose for the whole unit"},{"t":"p","x":"D. A set of inborn personality traits that cannot be learned"},{"t":"a","x":"**20. **B — Definition."},{"t":"p","x":"**21. **Path-goal theory: newly hired staff doing an unstructured task most need which style?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Directive"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Participative"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Achievement-oriented"},{"t":"a","x":"**21. **A — Unstructured task and novices: directive."},{"t":"p","x":"**22. **A team of highly experienced, self-motivated surgeons makes close task supervision unnecessary. This is an example of:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Romance of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Servant leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Leadership substitutes"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Charismatic leadership"},{"t":"a","x":"**22. **C — Experience substitutes for task-oriented leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"**23. **Profits rise because of industry-wide demand, but shareholders credit the CEO. This is the:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Romance of leadership effect"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Self-fulfilling prophecy"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Fundamental attribution error"},{"t":"a","x":"**23. **B — Romance of leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"**24. **Which is NOT one of the eight commonly identified leader attributes?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Integrity"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Emotional intelligence"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Introversion"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Knowledge of the business"},{"t":"a","x":"**24. **C — Introversion is not on the list."},{"t":"p","x":"**25. **Which pair belongs to the Dark Triad?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Extraversion and agreeableness"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Openness and neuroticism"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Narcissism and psychopathy"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Shyness and introversion"},{"t":"a","x":"**25. **C — Machiavellianism, narcissism, psychopathy."},{"t":"p","x":"**26. **A subordinate judges a female leader more harshly than a male leader for the same directive style. This illustrates:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. The path-goal model at work"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Servant leadership in practice"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Situational leadership theory"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Stereotypes distorting prototypes"},{"t":"a","x":"**26. **D — Prototypes and stereotypes shape evaluation."},{"t":"p","x":"**27. **An agent smiles at rude customers while angry inside (surface acting). The long-run risk is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Higher job satisfaction"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Emotional exhaustion"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Greater power"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Lower role ambiguity"},{"t":"a","x":"**27. **B — Emotional dissonance leads to exhaustion."},{"t":"p","x":"**28. **A manager is the only one who masters the key database and controls access to it. Her power effectiveness is increased mainly by:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Low visibility of her work to executives"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Easy substitution by other staff members"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Low centrality in the workflow of the firm"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Low substitutability"},{"t":"a","x":"**28. **D — Hard to substitute and central = stronger power."},{"t":"p","x":"**29. **Two departments fight over resources and each tries to win. This conflict-handling style is:"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Forcing"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Avoiding"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Yielding"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Problem solving"},{"t":"a","x":"**29. **A — Win-lose forcing."},{"t":"p","x":"**30. **Which action belongs to the first stage of Lewin's model?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Rewarding the new behaviour consistently"},{"t":"p","x":"B. Piloting the new process with a small team"},{"t":"p","x":"C. Creating urgency"},{"t":"p","x":"D. Embedding the change in the culture and the systems"},{"t":"a","x":"**30. **C — Unfreezing."}]},"BUS2205":{"title":"Mock Final Exam","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Last lesson of the course: a full mock final exam, built from the announced format, the briefing and the Moodle material."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Format"},{"t":"p","x":"Format: final case study plus essay (50% of the course), 2 hours, Thursday 10 December. Moodle gives no further detail on the paper, so this mock follows the format announced in class and covers every chapter taught: Kerin chapters 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9, 10, 11, 13, 14, 15, 18, 19 and 20. 100 marks: Part A case study (60 marks, compulsory, all questions); Part B essay (40 marks, choose ONE of three). Use the case facts and figures, define concepts briefly, apply them, and justify recommendations. Show calculations. Part C is extra drill."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"How to work it"},{"t":"li","x":"Open the Exercises tab. Work under exam conditions: closed book, time limit 2 hours."},{"t":"li","x":"Type your answer in the box (or on paper), then tap Show answer to see the model answer and marking guide. Mark yourself Got it or Missed."},{"t":"li","x":"Multiple choice is checked instantly."},{"t":"li","x":"Writing parts: paste your answer in the chat with Claude and ask for a correction against the marks."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Time plan"},{"t":"li","x":"Part A: about 70 minutes, six questions: roughly one minute per mark. Show calculations."},{"t":"li","x":"Part B: about 45 minutes, one essay with a thesis, frameworks, real examples and a counter-argument."},{"t":"li","x":"Use at least two real companies in the essay."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part A: Case study (60 marks, compulsory)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Case: Lantau Leaf, ready-to-drink tea (Hong Kong)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Company.** Lantau Leaf is a three-year-old Hong Kong company that sells cold-brewed, low-sugar ready-to-drink (RTD) teas in 330 ml glass bottles. Its mission is \"to make everyday tea healthier and more sustainable\". Founded by two former office workers, it has 22 employees, an office in Kwun Tong, and contracted production in Dongguan. Bottles are returned and refilled through a deposit scheme in 120 stores."},{"t":"p","x":"**Product range.** Jasmine green, oolong, and lemon black tea (core range), plus a seasonal \"Osmanthus\" flavour. Packaging is minimalist and carries a \"sugar-free, plastic-free\" claim."},{"t":"p","x":"**Sales and market.** Bottles sold: 180,000 (Year 1), 240,000 (Year 2), 330,000 (Year 3). Suggested retail price: HK$22. The Hong Kong RTD tea market was worth about HK$242 million in Year 3 at retail value. Competitors: large beverage groups (Vitasoy, Coca-Cola, Suntory) sell cheaper plastic bottled teas around HK$8 to HK$12; premium challengers sell at HK$18 to HK$28. A Japanese brand has announced entry next year."},{"t":"p","x":"**Distribution.** Lantau Leaf sells through a wholesaler to supermarkets, convenience stores and cafes. The **retailer margin is 30% of the retail price** and the **wholesaler margin is 15% of the wholesaler's selling price**. Unit variable cost (production, bottle, deposit processing, shipping to the wholesaler) is **HK$9**; fixed costs (office, salaries, marketing) are **HK$1.2 million per year**."},{"t":"p","x":"**Environment.** Hong Kong's plastic-bottle recycling is poor; the government plans to introduce a charge for disposable plastic items; consumers are more health-conscious, with high awareness of sugar content; average office worker salaries are rising; delivery apps and social media (Instagram, Xiaohongshu) drive discovery; sugar-labelling rules are being discussed."},{"t":"p","x":"**Research.** A recent survey of 400 Hong Kong consumers (online panel, 25 questions) found: 35% are health-conscious **office workers**; 25% are **students**; 15% are **tourists**; 25% are **families**. For the same product at HK$22, the share willing to buy at least weekly is 28% (office workers), 12% (students), 5% (tourists), 15% (families). In a price test, demand index 100 at HK$22 retail rose to 112 at HK$19.80. 62% of respondents said they \"like the idea\" of returnable bottles, but only 31% said they would return bottles regularly. An observation study in two convenience stores showed that shoppers spend about 6 seconds in front of the chilled drinks cabinet. Many comments on social media complain about bottles being \"hard to find\"."},{"t":"p","x":"**Customer data.** An office worker customer spends HK$600 per year on average, the gross margin is 40%, the average customer stays 4 years, and acquiring one costs HK$150."},{"t":"p","x":"**Management dilemma.** The founders discuss (1) a lower-priced \"Lantau Leaf Lite\" in plastic-free cartons; (2) expansion into 7-Eleven; (3) a social-media campaign with an influencer; (4) a 10% price cut to defend against the Japanese entrant."},{"t":"p","x":"Answer the six questions below using the case facts and figures."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Strategy and environment (10 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (a) State Lantau Leaf's **mission** and say what the three elements of the strategy (**organisational foundation, direction, strategies**) would be. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (b) Identify **two social, one economic, one technological, one competitive and one regulatory** force from the case and explain whether each is an opportunity or a threat. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (c) Using the **Ansoff (diversification) matrix**, classify each of the four management ideas (1) to (4) and identify the riskiest. Calculate Lantau Leaf's **dollar market share** (retail value) in Year 3. (3)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (3)** Mission: \"to make everyday tea healthier and more sustainable\" (the reason the company exists). **Organisational foundation** (why we exist): mission, vision, and values (health, sustainability, product quality). **Organisational direction** (what we will do): goals such as sales growth (330,000 to 400,000 bottles) and market share, and the businesses/segments. **Organisational strategies** (how we will do it): marketing mix, returnable-bottle system, channels, partnerships, the strategic marketing process of planning, implementation and evaluation. (Each element 1.)\n**(b) (4)** Social: health consciousness and sugar awareness (opportunity); recycling gaps and environmental concern (opportunity, but returning bottles needs habits); or generational shifts (young adults). Economic: rising office salaries (opportunity: more disposable income for a premium price); a downturn would threaten premium price. Technological: delivery apps, social media and e-commerce (opportunity); also refill tracking via apps. Competitive: large beverage groups (price competition, **substitutes**, brand competition: threat), premium challengers and the Japanese entrant (threat). Regulatory: plastic charge (opportunity for glass and returnable), sugar-labelling (opportunity: Lantau already low-sugar; threat if claims must meet standards). Each force 0.5 identification plus 0.5 opportunity/threat reasoning (cap 4).\n**(c) (3)** Ansoff: (1) Lantau Leaf Lite: **new product for a (partly) new market segment**: product development (riskier, new product and brand extension); (2) 7-Eleven: **existing product, new channel and customers**: market development; (3) influencer campaign: **market penetration**; (4) price cut: **market penetration**. Riskiest: (1) product development (new capability, cannibalisation, brand dilution), unless the answer argues diversification is the riskiest, but none of the four is true diversification (new product and new market). **Dollar market share** = Lantau sales ÷ industry sales = (330,000 × HK$22 = HK$7.26 million) ÷ HK$242 million = **3.0%**."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Consumer behaviour and research (10 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (a) Apply the **five-stage consumer purchase decision process** to an office worker choosing a drink at a convenience store, and say what type of **involvement** and problem solving applies. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (b) Use the 31%/62% gap and the six-second observation to explain **attitudes versus behaviour**, **situational influences**, and the role of **reference groups** and **culture** in Hong Kong. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (c) Using the **five-step marketing research approach**, outline a research plan to find out why customers do not return bottles (problem, plan, data collection with **primary and secondary** data, findings, action). Include two **question formats** you would use. (4)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (3)** Five stages: (1) **problem recognition** (thirst, a tired afternoon); (2) **information search** (internal memory, the cabinet, friends, social media); (3) **alternative evaluation** (price, sugar, bottle, brand); (4) **purchase decision**; (5) **post-purchase behaviour** (satisfaction, cognitive dissonance, loyalty, returning the bottle). Low-involvement, **routine or limited problem solving** for a HK$22 convenience purchase (six seconds), rising to **extended problem solving** the first time for a new premium brand. (Define involvement: personal, social, economic importance.)\n**(b) (3)** **Attitude-behaviour gap**: 62% like returnable bottles but only 31% say they would use them regularly: positive attitudes do not guarantee behaviour because of convenience cost and habits (usage barrier). **Situational influences** (purchase task, social surroundings, physical surroundings, temporal perspective, antecedent state): six seconds in front of the cabinet means time pressure and in-store cues decide. **Reference groups** (colleagues posting on social media; membership and aspirational groups) and **culture** (health and face/status consumption in Hong Kong, \"buy what peers buy\"; subculture of young professionals) shape choices; Maslow and perception also relevant.\n**(c) (4)** (1) **Define the problem**: low return rate (31%); objective: identify barriers and test solutions. (2) **Research plan**: **secondary data** (government recycling statistics, industry reports, existing customer database), **primary data**: observation of store behaviour, **focus groups** and a **survey**; sampling plan (office workers). (3) **Collect**: questionnaire with **closed-end** questions (dichotomous, multiple choice, rating or Likert scale) and an **open-end** question (\"what stops you from returning bottles?\"); avoid leading questions; pilot test. (4) **Develop findings**: analyse by segment, cross-tabulate, test reliability and validity. (5) **Take action**: more return points, app reminders, deposit incentives, evaluate by follow-up metrics."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Segmentation, targeting, positioning (10 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (a) Identify the **segmentation variables** used (demographic, behavioural, etc.) and explain which of the three **segmentation strategies** (undifferentiated, concentrated, differentiated) you would adopt. Use the survey figures to justify the **target market**. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (b) Calculate the **customer lifetime value** of an office worker customer net of acquisition cost, and interpret. (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (c) Write a **positioning statement** and describe a **perceptual map** (two axes, at least four brands) that would support it. (4)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (4)** Variables: **demographic** (occupation: office workers, students; age), **geographic** (Hong Kong residents versus tourists), **psychographic** (health-conscious), **behavioural** (usage rate: weekly buyers). Strategy: **concentrated** (one segment) or **differentiated** (several): recommend **concentrated** on office workers first because resources are small (22 staff). Survey evidence: weekly buyers as a percentage of all respondents: office workers 35% × 28% = **9.8%**; students 25% × 12% = 3.0%; tourists 15% × 5% = 0.75%; families 25% × 15% = 3.75%; total 17.3%, so **office workers are 9.8/17.3 = 57%** of weekly buyers: the largest and most valuable segment. Follow the five steps (segment buyers, group products, market-product grid, select targets, take marketing actions).\n**(b) (2)** CLV = annual spend × margin × years = 600 × 0.40 × 4 = **HK$960**; net of acquisition cost 150 = **HK$810**. Interpretation: each customer is worth about 5 times the cost of acquisition; it justifies loyalty spending and retention (CLV greater than acquisition cost, so the segment is profitable).\n**(c) (4)** Positioning statement (example): \"For health-conscious Hong Kong office workers who want a better everyday drink, Lantau Leaf is the low-sugar, refillable tea that is good for you and for the planet, unlike plastic bottled teas.\" Positioning approach: **product attributes and benefits** and use. **Perceptual map**: axes such as **price (low to high)** and **health/sustainability image (low to high)**; plot Lantau Leaf in the high health and high price quadrant; large beverage groups' plastic teas at low price and moderate health; premium challengers near; Japanese entrant. Find the gap and a position that is distinct, credible and valued. (Repositioning risk: perception must match reality.)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Products, brands and the life cycle (8 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (a) Classify the product (consumer product type) and explain the **new-product development** stages you would use for the Lite carton idea. List three **marketing reasons for new-product failure** and say how Lantau Leaf can avoid them. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (b) Where is the product in the **product life cycle** and the **diffusion of innovation** adoption categories? Name three **barriers to adoption** of returnable bottles (usage, value, risk, psychological). (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (c) Evaluate the **brand** and **packaging** decisions (brand equity, brand name, label, sustainability claims) and one ethical risk of \"plastic-free\" claims. (2)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (4)** Classification: **convenience good** (frequent, low-effort) but with **specialty/shopping** features due to the premium; a consumer **non-durable** good. **Seven stages** of new-product development: **new-product strategy development, idea generation, screening and evaluation, business analysis, development, market testing, commercialisation**. Apply to Lite: ideas from survey, screening against the strategy, business analysis (break-even and cannibalisation), prototype cartons, test market in 20 stores, then launch. **Marketing reasons for failure (any three)**: insignificant point of difference; incomplete market and product definition before development; poor execution of the marketing mix; poor positioning or messaging; not enough demand; poor timing; overestimating market size; not enough support from channel or insufficient funds. Avoidance: research first, differentiate (Lite must not look cheap), test, and secure channel support.\n**(b) (2)** The brand is in the **early introduction to early growth stage** (sales 180,000 to 330,000, +37.5% in the last year, a profit approaching, competitors entering). **Adopter categories**: innovators and early adopters (health-conscious urban professionals), with the early majority still to convince. **Barriers to adoption**: **usage** (does not fit habits; returning bottles takes effort), **value** (HK$22 price premium and deposit), **risk** (hygiene of reused glass), **psychological** (image or culture of convenience). Any three with explanation.\n**(c) (2)** **Brand equity**: awareness, perceived quality, associations, loyalty; the name should be memorable and meaningful (\"Lantau\" gives the nature association); packaging has protection, promotion, and sustainability functions; the label must be accurate. **Ethical risk**: \"plastic-free\" and \"sugar-free\" claims must be true (**greenwashing** and misleading claims law, brand damage); caps and labels may contain plastic, so the claim must be precise."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Pricing and channels (12 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (a) Calculate the price that Lantau Leaf **receives** (the manufacturer's selling price) from the HK$22 retail price. (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (b) Calculate the **unit contribution** and the **break-even quantity**, and the quantity needed to earn a **target profit of HK$800,000**. Compare the break-even quantity with the Year 3 sales. (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (c) At a sales volume of 400,000 bottles, calculate the **target return-on-sales price (20%)** and the **standard markup price (25% on selling price)** and say which pricing method is cost-, profit- or demand-oriented. (2)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (d) Evaluate idea (4): the 10% retail price cut. Calculate the **price elasticity of demand** (midpoint) from the price test, the effect on total retail revenue, and the effect on Lantau Leaf's unit contribution (the cut passes through the margins). Decide. (3)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (3)** Retail price 22; retailer margin 30% of the retail price, so the retailer pays 22 × (1 − 0.30) = **HK$15.40**. The wholesaler margin is 15% of its selling price (15.40), so the wholesaler pays 15.40 × (1 − 0.15) = **HK$13.09**. Lantau Leaf receives **HK$13.09** per bottle.\n**(b) (4)** Unit contribution = 13.09 − 9.00 = **HK$4.09**. Break-even = 1,200,000 / 4.09 = **293,399 bottles (about 293,400)**. For a target profit of 800,000: (1,200,000 + 800,000) / 4.09 = **488,998 bottles**. Year 3 sales of 330,000 are above break-even by about 36,600 bottles, a margin of safety of about 11%; the profit in Year 3 is 330,000 × 4.09 − 1,200,000 = **HK$149,700**; reaching the target profit needs 48% more volume.\n**(c) (2)** Unit cost at 400,000 bottles = 9 + 1,200,000 / 400,000 = **HK$12.00**. Target return-on-sales price = 12 / (1 − 0.20) = **HK$15.00** (profit-oriented). Standard markup price = 12 / (1 − 0.25) = **HK$16.00** (cost-oriented). (Demand-oriented methods use what customers will pay: here HK$22 retail.)\n**(d) (3)** Midpoint elasticity: %ΔQ = (112 − 100)/106 = +11.3%; %ΔP = (19.80 − 22)/20.90 = −10.5%; **PED = 1.08**, elastic (just). Retail revenue = 100 × 22 = 2,200 versus 112 × 19.80 = 2,217.6 (index), a rise of only **0.8%**. But the cut passes through the margins: retailer pays 19.80 × 0.70 = 13.86; wholesaler pays 13.86 × 0.85 = 11.78 so Lantau's **contribution = 11.78 − 9 = HK$2.78** (down 32%). Total contribution index: 112 × 2.78 = **311** versus 100 × 4.09 = **409**, a fall of 24%. **Reject the price cut** (or fund it only through lower margins) and compete on value and distribution instead."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Promotion and digital (10 marks)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (a) Design an **integrated marketing communications (IMC)** plan using the five promotional elements, with objectives (AIDA or hierarchy of effects), a message, and **two metrics** (dashboard). (4)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (b) Choose **media** for advertising, one **consumer sales promotion**, and a **public relations** action to handle the criticism that bottles are \"hard to find\". (3)"},{"t":"p","x":"• (c) Evaluate the **social media and influencer** idea: the **consumer's role** on social media, the owned/paid/earned media distinction, mobile marketing (geo-targeting, apps), the risks (reviews, authenticity), and how to measure success. (3)"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**(a) (4)** Five elements: **advertising, personal selling, sales promotion, public relations, direct marketing** (plus digital/social). IMC: one voice across all. Objectives: awareness (office workers), interest, desire and action (AIDA); e.g., raise awareness of the brand among office workers from X% to Y%, and increase the bottle-return rate from 31% to 45%. Message: \"Good for you, good for the planet\" with the refill story. Metrics (dashboard): reach and impressions, website and app traffic, cost per acquisition, **bottle return rate**, repeat purchase rate, social engagement, sales per store. The campaign steps: identify the target, set objectives, set the budget, design the program, evaluate.\n**(b) (3)** Media: out-of-home (MTR stations and office-building lifts), digital video and social ads, and in-store signage; criteria: reach, frequency, cost per thousand, fit with target. **Sales promotion** (consumer-oriented): coupons, sampling in office lobbies, deposit rewards, loyalty points, contests, premiums. **PR**: press release and events with a store map and \"find us\" app launch, partnerships with environmental NGOs or the Environmental Protection Department, community clean-up days, a response to the criticism (corrective messages, crisis management).\n**(c) (3)** Consumers act as **creators and co-producers** (user-generated content, reviews, hashtags); social media is two-way, so brands engage rather than broadcast. **Owned** (Instagram, website), **paid** (influencer, ads) and **earned** (shares, reviews) media. **Mobile**: geo-targeted offers near stores, QR codes on bottles, app with store finder and return rewards. Risks: **authenticity** (disclosed sponsorship, influencer mismatch), negative reviews and backlash, data privacy, loss of control. Measure: engagement rate, reach, sentiment, click-throughs, coupon redemptions, cost per acquisition, and return rate; compare against objectives."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part B: Essay (40 marks), answer ONE"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **Essay: \"Marketing creates value for customers and society, rather than simply creating wants.\" Discuss with reference to the marketing concept, relationship marketing, ethics and social responsibility, and use at least two real company examples (not Lantau Leaf)."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nStructure (all essays): introduction with a thesis (4); two or three well-developed arguments with frameworks and examples (24); counter-argument and evaluation (8); conclusion (4). Use real, dated examples (e.g., Patagonia for ethics and sustainability; Nike, Apple, Starbucks, Tesla, Oatly, Pop Mart, Shein for others) and avoid reproducing the Lantau case.\nMarketing concept (customer focus, integrated effort, profit through satisfaction), the four eras and **market orientation**, value creation (utility: form, place, time, possession), **relationship marketing/CRM**, and customer experience; critics: manufactured wants, targeting vulnerable consumers, greenwashing; **ethics and social responsibility** (societal marketing, CSR as a long-term investment, the triple bottom line); a balanced conclusion: value is created when firms deliver real benefits and are transparent, otherwise \"creating wants\" turns into manipulation."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **Essay: A well-known brand in the **maturity stage** of its life cycle faces both slowing sales and a social-media backlash on a sustainability issue. Using the product life cycle, branding, pricing, channels and IMC, recommend a strategy to revive its position. Support your answer with real examples."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nStructure (all essays): introduction with a thesis (4); two or three well-developed arguments with frameworks and examples (24); counter-argument and evaluation (8); conclusion (4). Use real, dated examples (e.g., Patagonia for ethics and sustainability; Nike, Apple, Starbucks, Tesla, Oatly, Pop Mart, Shein for others) and avoid reproducing the Lantau case.\n**Maturity**: product life extension (new uses, new users, more frequent use, product modification), repositioning, **branding** (brand extension, brand revitalisation), **pricing** (value pricing, promotions), **channels** (online, direct-to-consumer), **IMC** with authenticity; the backlash: acknowledge, correct actions, not just PR; examples (e.g., Coca-Cola's packaging changes, Nike's sustainability, Starbucks' cups). Evaluate costs, risks of harvesting versus deletion."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Essay: A premium Hong Kong brand wants to enter the Mainland China market through e-commerce and social commerce. Assess the opportunity using environmental scanning, consumer behaviour, STP, and the marketing mix (product, price, place, promotion), and identify the three main risks."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nStructure (all essays): introduction with a thesis (4); two or three well-developed arguments with frameworks and examples (24); counter-argument and evaluation (8); conclusion (4). Use real, dated examples (e.g., Patagonia for ethics and sustainability; Nike, Apple, Starbucks, Tesla, Oatly, Pop Mart, Shein for others) and avoid reproducing the Lantau case.\n**Environmental scan** of China (social: Gen Z, nationalism; economic: slowdown, consumption; technology: WeChat, Xiaohongshu, Douyin, Tmall/JD; competition: local premium brands; regulatory: advertising law, data law, imports); consumer behaviour (reference groups, KOLs, face); STP (cities, income, lifestyle); marketing mix (adaptation, pricing with duties and platform fees, place via cross-border e-commerce and flagship stores, promotion via livestream, KOLs); three risks: counterfeits, regulatory/political, platform dependence and fierce competition; recommend a phased entry."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Part C: Extra drill (10 short questions)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **Define a **marketing metric** and a **marketing dashboard**, and give two metrics for a café's loyalty scheme."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nA marketing metric is a measure of the quantitative value or trend of a marketing action or result; a marketing dashboard is a visual display of essential information on one screen. Loyalty metrics: repeat purchase rate, active members, redemption rate, average spend per member, retention."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **Explain the **BCG matrix** quadrants and name the strategy for each; in which quadrant would a new product with a small share in a fast-growing market sit?"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nBCG: **question mark** (high growth, low share: invest or divest), **star** (high growth, high share: invest), **cash cow** (low growth, high share: harvest/milk), **dog** (low growth, low share: divest). A new product with small share in a fast-growing market is a **question mark**."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Explain **Maslow's hierarchy** and give one marketing implication for a gym."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nPhysiological, safety, belonging, esteem, self-actualisation: gym messages: health and safety (physiological/safety), community classes (belonging), progress and status (esteem), personal best (self-actualisation); segment by need level."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **What is **perceptual selection** (selective exposure, comprehension, retention) and why does it matter for advertising?"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nPerception is the process of selecting, organising and interpreting stimuli: selective exposure (we notice some ads), comprehension (understand them our own way) and retention (remember little); advertisers must be relevant, repeat, and stand out."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Distinguish **markup on cost** from **markup on selling price**. A product costs HK$60 and the markup on selling price is 40%: what is the price?"},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nMarkup on cost = markup dollars ÷ cost; on selling price = markup dollars ÷ price. Price = 60 / (1 − 0.40) = **HK$100** (markup HK$40 = 40% of price = 66.7% of cost)."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **Describe the **seven stages of new-product development**."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nNew-product strategy, idea generation, screening and evaluation, business analysis, development, market testing, commercialisation."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **Distinguish a **fad**, a **fashion**, and a **high-learning** product."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nA **fad** has a very short life cycle (peaks quickly, vanishes), a **fashion** is a currently accepted style that recurs, a **high-learning** product requires significant consumer education before use (long introduction)."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **Describe **vertical marketing systems** (corporate, administered, contractual) and give one example each."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nCorporate (one firm owns channel stages, e.g., Apple stores), administered (a dominant member leads, e.g., Walmart and suppliers), contractual (franchises, e.g., McDonald's, wholesaler-sponsored groups)."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **Explain **push versus pull** promotion strategies."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\n**Push**: promote to channel members to push the product through (trade promotions, personal selling); **pull**: promote to final consumers to create demand that pulls the product (advertising, consumer promotions)."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **Explain the **ethical issues** in surge pricing and loss-leader pricing."},{"t":"a","x":"**Model answer.**\nSurge pricing: efficiency (matching supply and demand) versus price gouging in emergencies and perceived unfairness; loss leaders: below-cost prices may mislead customers and harm small competitors (and can be unlawful where predatory), but are lawful as a tactic if truthful and not designed to eliminate competitors."}]}};
